window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("strategy", {"topics":[{"key":"strategy/ai-roadmap","sources":[],"why":"- **AI projects fail on data, evaluation and adoption, not models.** The roadmap schedules those\n  constraints explicitly, because they are what actually stalls a use case. Agents add two more: whether\n  the tools (APIs with the right scopes) exist, and what a wrong action costs.\n- **The naive plan is a list.** Twenty workshop ideas, each championed by someone, none compared on the\n  same terms; the most exciting ones are staffed in parallel with promised dates.\n- **Three things then go wrong.**\n  - The exciting ideas often have the least ready data or the highest risk (customer-facing, regulated\n    decisions), so they stall in pilot.\n  - Every team rebuilds the same plumbing (model access, document ingestion, an eval set, logging), so\n    the fifth project costs as much as the first.\n  - The plan is pinned to one model or vendor; when capability or pricing moves a few months later, half\n    the plan is obsolete and the other half solved problems a new model now handles out of the box.\n- **A feature roadmap does not transfer.** In normal software, built to spec means it works. In AI you\n  can build exactly as designed and still be right only some of the time, which is not enough for the\n  workflow. Feasibility is an empirical question answered by an eval on real data.\n- **So schedule *finding out* before *scaling*.** Otherwise the organisation discovers in month nine that\n  a flagship use case cannot clear the accuracy bar, and the credibility loss poisons the next proposal.","how":"### 1. Build the candidate list from workflows, not technology\n- **Each candidate is a workflow step** with an owner, a volume, a current cost or cycle time, and a user\n  who would act on the output.\n- **Example framing:** \"triage inbound claims emails into twelve queues\", not \"use GenAI in claims\".\n- **Why:** value, baseline and adoption can only be measured on a workflow.\n- **Source:** use-case discovery (see the scoping module) produces the list; the roadmap is what you do\n  with it.\n\n### 2. Score on value and on feasibility separately\nValue is a small formula, not an adjective:\n\n```\nannual value ≈ volume × value per unit × capture rate × automation share − (run cost + review cost)\n```\n\n- **Capture rate is the part people skip.** Hours \"freed\" are worth nothing unless someone redeploys\n  them or a cost line actually moves.\n- **Automation share is unknown until the feasibility eval.** It is how much volume the system handles at\n  an acceptable error rate, so early value scores are ranges.\n- **Run cost is at production volume, not pilot volume.** A use case valuable at $0.02 per item can be\n  uneconomic at $0.50.\n\nFeasibility has independent axes, scored separately *because* a single gut-feel number hides which one\nwill kill the project:\n\n| Axis | Question |\n|---|---|\n| **Data readiness** | Does the input data exist, can you legally use it, can you get access this quarter? |\n| **Evaluability** | Can you build a labelled set and define \"correct\"? If experts disagree on the answer, you cannot prove the system works. |\n| **Error tolerance** | What does a wrong output cost, and is there a human checkpoint? Drafting with review tolerates errors that autonomous decisions do not. |\n| **Risk class** | Regulatory, privacy and reputational exposure (feeds the AI inventory and risk tiering). |\n| **Integration effort** | Read-only assistance is cheap; writing back into systems of record is not. |\n| **Tool readiness (agents)** | Do APIs exist for every action the agent needs, with scoped credentials, or must someone build them first? |\n| **Action reversibility (agents)** | Can a wrong tool call be undone (a draft, a ticket comment) or not (a refund, an email sent)? |\n\n- **For an agent, the weakest axis is often tool readiness.** A great model with no write API to the\n  claims system is a chatbot, not an agent, so the missing API becomes a foundation on the roadmap.\n\n### 3. Sequence for capability, not just for score\n- **Early projects:** high-feasibility, moderate-value, and they *build reusable assets*: an access path\n  to the document store, an evaluation harness, a gateway with logging, a review UI.\n- **Later projects:** higher-value but riskier, starting with those assets in place.\n- **So the top-scoring idea is not always first.** If it needs four foundations nobody has built, it goes\n  second or third, behind a project that builds two of them.\n\n### 4. Decide build, buy or wait for each item\n| Decision | When |\n|---|---|\n| **Buy** | A vendor product already does it acceptably. |\n| **Build** | It needs your data and workflow integration. |\n| **Wait** | The blocker is raw model capability that the market is improving quickly. |\n\n- **\"Wait\" is legitimate** *because* elaborate workarounds for a capability gap are often wasted when the\n  next model release closes it.\n- **A waiting item needs a trigger**, such as \"re-run the feasibility eval whenever a new model in our\n  approved set ships\", or it is just a polite way of dropping it.\n\n### 5. Attach a gate to every stage\n1. Stages: discovery → feasibility eval → pilot → production → scale.\n2. Each transition has a pre-agreed criterion and a kill condition. Example: \"proceed to pilot if the\n   offline eval on 300 labelled cases meets the agreed accuracy bar at the agreed cost per item; stop if\n   after two iterations it is still far below.\"\n3. **Gates make cancelling cheap and normal**, *because* the decision rule was set before anyone was\n   invested in the answer.\n4. **The economics favour this:** a feasibility eval costs weeks and a production build costs months, so\n   the early gate buys information cheaply before the expensive commitment.\n\nWhat each gate measures changes as the item matures:\n\n| Gate | Checks |\n|---|---|\n| Feasibility | Offline accuracy and cost per item |\n| Pilot | Whether users actually act on the output, and whether the workflow metric moved |\n| Production | Cost at full volume, latency, monitoring, and an owner on call |\n\n### 6. Plan in horizons and re-plan on a cadence\n- **Near term (this quarter):** committed scope and dates.\n- **Mid term:** ranked candidates with the foundations they need.\n- **Long term:** themes and bets, explicitly unscheduled.\n- **Review quarterly, and re-score whenever the model landscape moves.** A capability jump can promote a\n  parked use case; a price drop can flip one from uneconomic to obvious.\n- **Commit to *capabilities required*** (\"extract fields from scanned forms at this accuracy\"), not to a\n  named model, so the roadmap survives model churn.\n\n### 7. Make the portfolio visible\n- **A living artefact:** each row has owner, stage, gate criterion, latest eval result, cost per item, and\n  next decision date.\n- **Governance reuses it:** this is also what the AI inventory needs.\n- **For agents, add an autonomy column** so reviewers see at a glance which agents act without a human.\n\n### 8. Gate autonomy, not just accuracy (agents)\nAn agent's roadmap row climbs an autonomy ladder, and each rung is its own gate:\n\n| Rung | The agent | Gate evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Draft | Proposes actions; a human executes them | Offline task-completion rate on real tasks, cost per task |\n| Act with approval | Executes after a human approves each consequential tool call | Approval rate, how often reviewers edit or reject the proposed call |\n| Act alone (low-risk actions) | Executes reversible actions without review | Production error rate on those actions, rollback path, an owner on call |\n\n- **Why a ladder:** the cost of a wrong action changes per rung, so the evidence bar must too.\n- **Reversible actions climb first**, because a mistake there costs a correction, not an incident.","example":"### Sequencing a portfolio by score and foundations\nA scoring pass over a small portfolio. Scores are 1–5 judgements from workshops; the numbers are\nillustrative, the mechanics are the point.\n\n```python\ncands = {  # value, data, evaluability, error_tolerance, needs (foundations)\n    \"claims_email_triage\": (3, 5, 5, 4, {\"gateway\", \"eval_harness\"}),\n    \"contract_clause_qa\":  (4, 4, 3, 3, {\"gateway\", \"eval_harness\", \"doc_ingest\"}),\n    \"auto_claim_decision\": (5, 2, 2, 1, {\"gateway\", \"eval_harness\", \"doc_ingest\", \"review_ui\"}),\n    \"agent_reply_drafts\":  (3, 4, 3, 4, {\"gateway\", \"review_ui\"}),\n}\ndef feasibility(d, e, t):\n    return min(d, e, t)          # the weakest axis is what kills a project, so use min, not mean\n\nbuilt, plan = set(), []\nremaining = dict(cands)\nwhile remaining:\n    def priority(item):\n        v, d, e, t, needs = item[1]\n        new_work = len(needs - built)            # foundations this project would have to build\n        return v * feasibility(d, e, t) - 2 * new_work\n    name, (v, d, e, t, needs) = max(remaining.items(), key=priority)\n    plan.append((name, v * feasibility(d, e, t), sorted(needs - built)))\n    built |= needs\n    del remaining[name]\n\nfor step, (name, score, builds) in enumerate(plan, 1):\n    print(step, name, \"score\", score, \"builds\", builds)\n# 1 claims_email_triage score 12 builds ['eval_harness', 'gateway']\n# 2 contract_clause_qa score 12 builds ['doc_ingest']\n# 3 agent_reply_drafts score 9 builds ['review_ui']\n# 4 auto_claim_decision score 5 builds []\n```\n\n- **The highest-value item comes last.** Automated claim decisions have error tolerance 1, which caps\n  feasibility; by the time it starts, every foundation it needs already exists.\n- **Contract Q&A beats reply drafting for second place.** Once the gateway and harness exist, each needs\n  exactly one new foundation (doc ingestion vs. review UI), so the tie-break is the base score: 12\n  against 9.\n- **`min` rather than an average is deliberate.** Excellent data does not rescue a use case nobody can\n  evaluate.\n- **Check the plan is not an artefact of the weights.** The penalty of 2 points per new foundation is a\n  judgement call; here any weight from 1 to 4 gives the same order, which makes the sequence defensible\n  in the meeting.\n- **If a small weight change reshuffles the plan**, the scores are too close to decide on, and the honest\n  move is a quick feasibility eval on the tied items.\n\n### Worked value range: why run cost at production volume matters\nApplying the value formula to the claims-email triage item. All inputs are illustrative.\n\n| Input | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Volume | 120,000 emails per year |\n| Value per unit (handling time saved) | $2.00 |\n| Capture rate (time actually redeployed) | 0.6 |\n| Review cost | $15,000 per year |\n\n| Scenario | Automation share | Run cost | Annual value |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pessimistic | 0.5 | $0.02 × 120,000 = $2,400 | 120,000 × 2 × 0.6 × 0.5 − 17,400 = $54,600 |\n| Expected | 0.7 | $2,400 | 100,800 − 17,400 = $83,400 |\n| Optimistic | 0.8 | $2,400 | 115,200 − 17,400 = $97,800 |\n| Expected, at $0.50 per item | 0.7 | $60,000 | 100,800 − 75,000 = $25,800 |\n\n- **The score is a range** (roughly $55k to $98k) until the feasibility eval pins automation share.\n- **Price per item moves the answer more than accuracy here:** the same use case loses about 70% of its\n  value at $0.50 per item, which is why run cost is priced at production volume.\n\n### Worked scenario: single call or agent at the feasibility gate\nThe same triage item, evaluated on 300 labelled tasks two ways: one model call per email, or an agent\nthat looks up the policy and claim history with tools before routing. Same volume, value per unit,\ncapture rate and review cost as above; all numbers are illustrative.\n\n| Variant | Completion (automation share) | Cost per task | Run cost per year | Annual value |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Single call | 0.72 | $0.01 | $1,200 | 103,680 − 16,200 = $87,480 |\n| Agent, ~6 tool steps | 0.88 | $0.06 | $7,200 | 126,720 − 22,200 = $104,520 |\n| Agent that loops to ~20 steps | 0.88 | $0.20 | $24,000 | 126,720 − 39,000 = $87,720 |\n\n- **The agent earns its place only with a step budget.** Better completion is worth about $17k a year\n  here, and an unbounded loop spends all of it on tokens.\n- **So the gate records cost per task, not cost per call**, and the roadmap row carries the step budget.","patterns":["**Portfolio register as one table** — for any organisation with more than a handful of candidates, keep one sheet or tracker with owner, stage, gate criterion, latest eval result, cost per item and next decision date. Monitor items with no eval result in the last quarter; they are drifting, not progressing.","**Pre-registered gates** — before a feasibility eval runs, write the pass bar, the eval set size (for example 300 labelled cases) and the kill condition into the item's record. Monitor the kill rate per quarter; zero means the gates are not being applied.","**Model-release re-score trigger** — for every \"wait\" item, store the feasibility eval as a re-runnable job and re-run it when a model joins the approved set. Monitor how many parked items get promoted or formally dropped.","**Weight sensitivity check** — before presenting a ranking, re-run it across a plausible range of weights (as with the 1 to 4 foundation penalty above). If the order changes, flag the tied items for a quick eval rather than a debate.","**Baseline before pilot** — measure the current workflow's cycle time, cost per item and error rate before the pilot starts, so the pilot gate can say by how much the system beats the old process.","**Autonomy level per agent row** — record the agent's current rung (draft, act with approval, act alone) and the evidence needed for the next one. Monitor reviewer edit and reject rates on proposed tool calls; promote only when they stay low over an agreed window.","**Agent eval suite as the gate artefact** — each agent row links to a re-runnable trajectory eval on real tasks that reports completion rate, tool-error rate and cost per task. Monitor that every gate decision cites a run of it, not a demo."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The roadmap decides which agent you build first, how much autonomy it\n  starts with, and which shared pieces (model gateway, tool layer, eval harness, approval UI) it must\n  leave behind. A badly sequenced roadmap means every agent team rebuilds its own tool wrappers and evals.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** As gate criteria you must meet: a task-completion bar on an\n  offline agent eval, a cost per task at production volume, and an autonomy level (draft, act with\n  approval, act alone) written into the roadmap row.\n- **What you control.** How fast you turn a roadmap row into a re-runnable agent eval on real tasks, and\n  reporting results in the gate's terms (completion rate, cost per task, tool-error rate) so go or stop is\n  obvious.\n- **What you can skip.** Portfolio-finance detail and long-horizon theme planning; know the value formula,\n  the feasibility axes and the gate idea well enough to argue for or against an agent project.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Choosing the first agent.** A team with ten agent ideas picks the one with ready tools and reversible\n  actions, so its eval harness and tool layer carry the next three.\n- **Promoting an agent's autonomy.** A support agent moves from drafting replies to sending low-risk ones\n  only after reviewer reject rates stay low for the agreed window.\n- **Parking an agent on a capability gap.** A document-heavy agent that misses the bar waits with a\n  trigger to re-run its eval when a new model joins the approved set.\n- **Defending an agent budget.** Cost per task at production volume, not a demo, decides whether an agent\n  beats a single model call.","mini_project":"**Score and gate three agent ideas (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn three agent ideas into roadmap rows with a feasibility verdict backed by a small eval.\n- **Steps:** write each idea as a workflow step with volume and value per unit; score every feasibility\n  axis, including tool readiness and action reversibility; for the top one, build a 30-task eval and a tiny\n  tool-calling agent with two mock tools; log completion rate, steps and cost per task; apply the value\n  formula with a pessimistic and an expected automation share.\n- **Done when:** you have a portfolio table with a build, buy or wait call per idea, the agent eval result\n  in the gate's terms, a step budget, and a stated autonomy rung with the evidence needed for the next."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"Discovery: workflow candidate\"] --> S[\"Score value and feasibility\"]\n    S --> B{\"Build, buy or wait?\"}\n    B -->|\"wait\"| W[\"Parked with re-eval trigger\"]\n    W -.->|\"new model ships\"| F\n    B -->|\"build or buy\"| F[\"Agent eval on real tasks\"]\n    F -->|\"meets bar\"| P[\"Pilot: agent acts with approval\"]\n    F -->|\"kill condition\"| K[\"Stopped\"]\n    P -->|\"workflow metric moved\"| PR[\"Production: full-volume cost, owner\"]\n    P -->|\"no adoption\"| K\n    PR --> SC[\"Scale\"]\n    PR -.->|\"builds foundations\"| S","caption":"Follow one agent use case through its stages: every arrow forward is a gate with a pre-agreed\ncriterion, every stage has an exit to stop or wait, and the pilot is where the agent first acts."},"interview":[{"q":"How is an AI roadmap different from an ordinary software feature roadmap?","a":"- **Feasibility is empirical.** A system built exactly to spec can still be right only some of the time,\n  so the roadmap schedules a feasibility eval on real data before it schedules scaling.\n- **Items are hypotheses with gates.** Each stage has a pre-agreed pass criterion and kill condition,\n  and the plan commits to capabilities rather than named models because the model market moves faster\n  than the planning cycle."},{"q":"Your portfolio has eight items \"in progress\" and none has an eval result from the last quarter. What do you do?","a":"- **Diagnose it as missing gates.** Items that never stop and only lose staff mean kill criteria were\n  never agreed or never applied.\n- **Force a decision per item:** write the gate criterion now, run the cheapest feasibility eval on real\n  data within weeks, and stop or park items that miss the bar.\n- **Then track the portfolio:** time to feasibility verdict, share of items with a recent gate decision,\n  and the kill rate."},{"q":"When would you put the highest-value use case first, against the usual sequencing advice?","a":"- **When funding depends on it.** If one case is so dominant that the programme only gets funded by\n  attacking it, delaying it can kill the whole roadmap.\n- **Even then, gate it.** Start with a feasibility eval and a human-in-the-loop variant, and build the\n  foundations it needs as part of the project rather than assuming they exist.\n- **The cost** is a higher chance of a visible stall, so set expectations with explicit evidence gates."},{"q":"How do you estimate an agent use case's value before you know its task-completion rate?","a":"- **Use the formula:** volume × value per unit × capture rate × automation share − (run cost + review\n  cost). For an agent, automation share is the completion rate on real tasks and run cost is cost per\n  task (all steps and retries), not per call.\n- **Report a range.** Automation share is unknown until the feasibility eval, so give pessimistic and\n  expected values, and price run cost at production volume, since $0.02 versus $0.50 per item can flip\n  the answer.\n- **Discount freed hours** by a capture rate unless someone will actually redeploy them."},{"q":"What does \"wait\" mean on a roadmap, and how do you stop it becoming a silent drop?","a":"- **Wait is for capability gaps** the market is closing quickly, because workarounds built now are often\n  wasted when the next model release ships.\n- **Attach a trigger:** keep the feasibility eval re-runnable and re-run it whenever a new model joins\n  the approved set, with an owner and a review date on the item."},{"q":"A sponsor wants the support agent to issue refunds on its own next quarter. How do you put that on the roadmap?","a":"- **As an autonomy ladder, not a date.** Start with the agent drafting refunds for a human, then acting\n  with per-call approval, then acting alone only below a refund amount where mistakes are cheap.\n- **Each rung has a gate:** offline completion on real tickets, then reviewer approve and edit rates,\n  then a production error rate with a rollback path and an owner on call.\n- **Foundations first:** a scoped refund API, an approval UI and audit logging are roadmap items too."}],"tradeoffs":["**Feasible first vs headline value first.** Starting with modest, feasible use cases builds foundations and credibility, but delays the headline value; it flips when one high-value case is so dominant that funding depends on attacking it first.","**Capabilities vs named models.** Committing to capabilities keeps the plan durable, but makes budgeting vaguer; once a use case is in production, pin the model and price it precisely.","**Many parallel pilots vs few sequential ones.** Parallel pilots surface feasibility fast, but spread scarce expert labellers and platform engineers thin; with a small team, run two or three sequentially with hard gates.","**Early foundations vs unused platform.** Shared foundations lower marginal cost later, but risk a platform with no users; build each foundation only when a scheduled use case needs it.","**Agent vs single model call.** An agent with tools lifts completion on messy tasks but multiplies cost and failure points per task; if a single call already clears the gate, the agent is not worth it yet."],"pitfalls":["**Ranking by excitement or executive sponsorship alone.** Symptom: a pilot graveyard where several projects sit for months at \"almost good enough\". Fix: score value and feasibility separately.","**No kill criteria at gates.** Projects never stop, they quietly lose staff, and the portfolio shows many items \"in progress\" with no recent eval result. Fix: pre-agreed kill conditions.","**Counting freed hours as value without a plan to redeploy them.** Finance later finds no cost moved and the next roadmap gets less funding. Fix: include capture rate.","**Pinning the plan to one model's capabilities.** When a new release changes what is easy, the team keeps building workarounds the new model makes unnecessary. Fix: commit to capabilities.","**Skipping baseline measurement before deployment.** The launch review looks good but nobody can say by how much it beats the old process, so the next item gets no funding.","**Treating a pilot pass as production readiness.** Costs and error rates jump at full volume or on input types the hand-picked pilot users never sent, and the item slides back to \"almost done\".","**Scheduling full autonomy as a date.** The roadmap says \"agent auto-approves refunds in Q3\" and the team ships it without approval-rate evidence; the first wrong refunds end the programme. Fix: gate each autonomy rung on measured evidence, reversible actions first."],"when":"- **Use when:** there are more candidate use cases than people to build them, which is almost immediately.\n- **Keep it short-horizon and evidence-driven:** the quarter's commitments in detail, the rest as ranked\n  hypotheses; review quarterly or when the model market moves.\n- **Measure each item** with its gate metric: offline eval score, cost per item, adoption in pilot. For\n  agents: task-completion rate, cost per task and the reviewer reject rate at the current autonomy rung.\n- **Measure the portfolio:** time from idea to feasibility verdict, the share of items with a gate decision\n  in the last quarter, and the kill rate. A kill rate of zero is not a sign of good ideas; it means the\n  gates are not being applied.\n- **Avoid:** a three-year plan of dated AI features; it will be wrong within months and will make every\n  later change look like failure.\n- **Interviews:** a strong answer separates value from feasibility, names evaluability and data access as\n  the real constraints, and explains how sequencing builds reusable capability.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns a roadmap item into a feasibility eval on real data quickly, and reports results in the gate's terms so the go/stop call is obvious.","architect":"Owns sequencing and the foundation list: which shared capabilities each stage builds, and which risk tier each use case sits in.","fde":"Runs discovery inside the customer, scores their candidates honestly, and steers them away from the flagship-but-infeasible use case toward a first win that unlocks the rest."},"check":{"q":"An executive wants the roadmap to lead with fully automated loan decisions because it has the\nlargest projected savings. How do you respond?","a":"I would not refuse it, I would sequence it. Automated credit decisions score high on value but low\non error tolerance and high on regulatory risk, and they need foundations we lack: document ingestion, an\neval set agreed by credit experts, an audit trail and a review UI. I would propose a first stage that\nbuilds those through a lower-risk adjacent use case, such as extracting and summarising applications for\nunderwriters, with a gate: if extraction accuracy on a few hundred labelled files meets the bar, proceed\nto decision support with a human approving, and only consider autonomy once the error profile is measured\nin production. The savings case stays on the roadmap, with evidence gates instead of a date."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why score feasibility as the minimum of its axes rather than their average?","options":["Averages are harder to compute consistently across workshops","A single blocking constraint, such as no way to evaluate correctness, stops a project regardless of strengths elsewhere","The minimum always produces a higher priority for high-value projects","Averaging would double-count the value score"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Feasibility constraints are conjunctive: excellent data cannot compensate for an output nobody can verify. An average lets one strong axis mask the one that will actually stall the project in pilot."},{"q":"What is the main reason an AI roadmap should commit to required capabilities rather than named models?","options":["Named models are not allowed in governance documents","Capabilities are easier to explain to executives","Model capability and pricing change faster than the planning cycle, so model-pinned plans go stale","Vendors do not allow customers to plan around specific model versions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Over a planning horizon, new releases change what is easy and what it costs. Stating the capability and its bar lets any model that clears it be used, while production items still pin a specific model."},{"q":"A use case has the highest value score but needs four foundations nobody has built. What does good sequencing usually do?","options":["Start it first because value dominates every other factor","Drop it from the roadmap as infeasible","Schedule it after projects that build those foundations, so it starts with them in place","Build all four foundations as a standalone platform project before any use case"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sequencing for capability lets cheaper, feasible projects build the shared assets while producing value. Building foundations with no consuming use case risks an unused platform; dropping it discards the value."}]},{"key":"strategy/platform-strategy","sources":[],"why":"- **The same needs recur.** Past a couple of AI projects, every team needs an API key to a model\n  provider, prompt and cost logging, document ingestion, an eval set and harness, PII redaction and a\n  review UI. Agent teams add tool wrappers with credentials and an approval step. Left alone, each team\n  builds its own.\n- **Duplication has visible costs:** duplicated engineering, and several contracts with the same provider\n  at worse prices.\n- **And invisible ones:** no single inventory of where models are used, no consistent data-retention\n  policy, no way to rotate a key or swap a deprecated model everywhere, no organisation-wide view of\n  spend.\n- **The opposite extreme also fails.** A central team designs a comprehensive \"AI platform\" up front and\n  mandates it. It is designed from guesses, so it fits nobody exactly; built before anyone uses it, so\n  there is no feedback; and as the only sanctioned path, every missing feature becomes a ticket while\n  teams wait. Teams route around it, and you have both a platform and shadow stacks.\n- **Platform strategy sits between them.** It decides the boundary, timing, operating model and funding\n  so the shared layer is pulled by demand, not pushed by architecture. Build-vs-buy decides how each layer\n  is sourced; centralised-vs-federated decides ownership; this lesson covers the sequencing and economics\n  that tie them together.","how":"### 1. Inventory the recurring capabilities\n- **List what each current and roadmapped use case needs, layer by layer:** model access, routing and\n  fallback, secrets, prompt and model registry, retrieval and ingestion, evaluation, observability and\n  cost attribution, guardrails, human review.\n- **For agents, add the agent-specific layers:** tool access to internal systems, tool credentials and\n  scopes, trajectory tracing, per-run step and spend budgets, approval gates, and sandboxes for code tools.\n- **The inventory is the demand signal.** Without it, the platform is designed from imagination.\n\n### 2. Score each capability on three questions\n| Question | What to ask | Implication |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Breadth of demand** | How many teams need it now or in the next two quarters? | Common heuristic: wait until the third consumer, *because* by then you have seen enough variation to generalise correctly. |\n| **Variance between teams** | Roughly the same thing (a gateway, audit logging) or genuinely different (retrieval tuned to legal contracts vs. support tickets)? | Low-variance capabilities centralise well; high-variance ones become a lowest-common-denominator abstraction every team fights. |\n| **Externalities** | Does getting it wrong in one team create risk or cost for everyone (leaked keys, data in an unapproved region, uncapped spend, no audit trail)? | These belong in the shared layer even with low demand, *because* the cost of inconsistency is organisational. |\n\n### 3. Check the economics before you centralise\nWith *n* teams each spending *c_team* to build and run their own version, and a platform costing\n*C_platform* to build and run plus *c_adopt* per team to integrate and adapt:\n\n```\ncentralise when  n × c_team  >  C_platform + n × c_adopt + coordination cost\n                 (+ the expected cost of an inconsistency incident, for high-externality items)\n```\n\n- **Variance is what breaks it.** When needs differ, *c_adopt* rises toward *c_team* (every team still does\n  most of the work, now fighting an abstraction), and the inequality never holds however large *n* gets.\n- **Externalities enter as the last term.** That is why a gateway or audit log can justify itself at\n  *n* = 2 while a shared chunking library cannot at *n* = 6.\n- **The same arithmetic covers sourcing.** If a commercial or open-source gateway gives you most of\n  *C_platform* for less, the platform team's job is to operate and integrate it, not to write one.\n\n### 4. Draw a thin mandatory core and an optional paved road\n- **Mandatory core:** what every AI workload must go through: identity, approved model access through a\n  gateway, budgets and rate limits, logging to a common trace store, data-classification rules. Mandatory\n  *because* its value comes from being universal (one inventory, one place to swap a model).\n- **Paved road:** everything else offered as defaults (an eval harness, an ingestion template, a review\n  UI). Optional, but easier than building your own.\n- **Off-road teams own what they build.**\n\n### 5. Treat the platform as a product\n- **It has users** (internal teams), a roadmap driven by their needs, documentation and SLOs.\n- **Adoption metrics:** number of teams onboarded, time from idea to first production call, share of\n  spend flowing through the gateway.\n- **Measure outcomes, not output.** A platform team measured on features shipped drifts toward the\n  over-built monolith described above.\n\n### 6. Choose a funding model\n| Model | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| **Central funding** | Makes adoption easy but hides cost. |\n| **Showback** (reporting each team's usage) | Creates awareness without friction. |\n| **Chargeback** (billing teams) | Aligns incentives, but can push teams to route around the platform to save money. |\n\nMany organisations start with central funding plus showback, and move to chargeback for variable costs\nsuch as tokens once usage is significant.\n\n### 7. Keep seams so decisions are reversible\n- **Expose stable internal interfaces:** an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, a trace schema, an eval-result\n  format.\n- **Do not leak a vendor's SDK into every service**, *because* the model and tooling market changes\n  quickly and you will swap components under the interface.\n\n### 8. Engineer the core as shared infrastructure\n- **A mandatory gateway is on every AI request path**, so its failure is everyone's outage.\n- **Run it at the availability target of the most demanding consumer**, stateless and horizontally\n  scaled.\n- **Decide the down-mode up front:** fail closed for high-risk traffic, fail open to a direct route only\n  where policy allows.\n- **Why:** the centralisation that gives you one place to swap a model also gives you one place to break\n  all of them.\n\n### 9. Where agent capabilities usually land\n| Agent capability | Variance | Externality | Usual home |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Tool credentials and scopes for internal systems | low | high (over-scoped access, leaked secrets) | mandatory core |\n| Trajectory traces in one schema | low | high (audit, incident replay) | mandatory core |\n| Per-run step and spend budgets | low | high (runaway loops burn shared spend) | mandatory core |\n| Shared tool servers for common systems (CRM, tickets) | medium | medium | paved road |\n| Approval-gate service and review UI | medium | medium | paved road |\n| Agent loop, planning, prompts, tool selection | high | low | team-owned |\n\n- **The pattern:** what an agent is *allowed* to touch and spend is central; how it *decides* stays local.","example":"### Break-even calculator for centralising a capability\nThe inequality from step 3 as code. Costs are $k per year and illustrative; the point is how variance\nand externalities move the answer.\n\n```python\ndef teams_cost(n, c_team, incident=0):\n    return n * c_team + incident          # every team builds and runs its own, plus expected incident cost\n\ndef platform_cost(n, C_platform, c_adopt, coord):\n    return C_platform + n * c_adopt + coord\n\nitems = {  # c_team, C_platform, c_adopt, coord, incident   ($k per year, illustrative)\n    \"gateway+budgets\":  (60, 150, 10, 20, 200),\n    \"gateway, no risk\": (60, 150, 10, 20, 0),\n    \"chunking library\": (40, 120, 35, 20, 0),\n}\nfor name, (c_team, C, c_adopt, coord, inc) in items.items():\n    for n in (2, 6):\n        t, p = teams_cost(n, c_team, inc), platform_cost(n, C, c_adopt, coord)\n        print(f\"{name:16} n={n}: teams {t:>3} vs platform {p:>3} ->\",\n              \"centralise\" if t > p else \"leave with teams\")\n# gateway+budgets  n=2: teams 320 vs platform 190 -> centralise\n# gateway+budgets  n=6: teams 560 vs platform 230 -> centralise\n# gateway, no risk n=2: teams 120 vs platform 190 -> leave with teams\n# gateway, no risk n=6: teams 360 vs platform 230 -> centralise\n# chunking library n=2: teams  80 vs platform 210 -> leave with teams\n# chunking library n=6: teams 240 vs platform 350 -> leave with teams\n```\n\n- **The gateway clears break-even at n = 2 only because of the incident term**; without it, it needs more\n  consumers.\n- **The chunking library never clears it**, because `c_adopt` (35) is close to `c_team` (40): high\n  variance means each team still does most of the work.\n\n### Worked scenario: an insurer with six product teams\nThe platform lead tabulates the capabilities (team counts come from the inventory; the scenario is\nillustrative):\n\n| Capability | Teams needing it | Variance | Externality | Decision |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Model access + keys + budgets | 6 | low | high (spend, data region) | mandatory core |\n| Prompt/response logging, cost attribution | 6 | low | high (audit) | mandatory core |\n| PII redaction before the model | 4 | low | high (privacy) | mandatory core |\n| Eval harness + result store | 5 | medium | medium | paved road |\n| Document ingestion pipeline | 3 | medium | low | paved road, template |\n| Retrieval strategy / chunking | 3 | high | low | team-owned |\n| Agent framework | 2 | high | low | team-owned, revisit |\n\n1. **Before:** each team had its own provider account; finance saw six invoices and no per-feature\n   breakdown; security found two teams sending claim documents to a model region the data policy did not\n   allow.\n2. **First release, deliberately small:** a gateway in front of approved models, with per-team budgets,\n   common logging and redaction.\n3. **Cheap migration:** the gateway speaks the same API shape teams already call.\n4. **Within a quarter:** all spend is attributable per feature; one team's model deprecation is handled by\n   changing a routing rule once that team's evals pass on the replacement model.\n5. **Next:** the eval harness, because five teams asked for it.\n6. **Agent frameworks stay team-owned:** only two teams use them and their needs differ, so a shared\n   abstraction now would freeze the wrong design.\n\n### Worked scenario: shared tool access for agent teams\nTwo, then four, agent teams each wrap the CRM API as a tool with their own service account. Plug the\nbreak-even inequality in with c_team 30, C_platform 60, c_adopt 5 and coordination 10 ($k per year,\nillustrative); the incident term (50) is the expected cost of an over-scoped credential letting an agent\nwrite where it should only read.\n\n| Case | Teams cost (n × c_team + incident) | Platform cost (C + n × c_adopt + coord) | Decision |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| n=2, incident 50 | 60 + 50 = 110 | 60 + 10 + 10 = 80 | centralise |\n| n=2, no incident term | 60 | 80 | leave with teams |\n| n=4, incident 50 | 120 + 50 = 170 | 60 + 20 + 10 = 90 | centralise |\n\n- **Same shape as the gateway:** least-privilege tool credentials justify a shared tool server early,\n  because one agent's mis-scoped key is everyone's audit finding.\n- **What each team keeps:** which tools its agent sees, the tool descriptions in its prompt, and its loop.","patterns":["**Gateway with per-team keys and budgets** — as the first core release, put an OpenAI-compatible proxy (open-source such as LiteLLM's proxy, or a commercial gateway) in front of approved models, issue one key per team or workload with a spend limit, and block direct provider keys. Monitor share of spend through the gateway and provider invoices that map to no registered workload.","**Showback from request metadata** — require team and feature tags on every gateway request, write them into the common trace store, and publish a monthly per-feature cost report before considering chargeback. Monitor the share of untagged spend.","**Model aliases as the swap seam** — services call a stable alias (for example \"extraction-default\") that the gateway maps to a concrete model version; change the mapping only after the consuming team's evals pass. Monitor error rate and cost per alias after each remap.","**Tier-one gateway operations** — run the gateway stateless with multiple replicas across zones, with a documented fail-closed or fail-open rule per traffic class. Monitor availability, added p99 latency and how often teams request bypass routes.","**Exception register for off-road teams** — any team leaving the paved road records owner, reason and a review date. Monitor the count; a rising number means the paved road no longer fits.","**Per-run budgets for agents** — tag each gateway request with an agent run ID and cap steps or spend per run, enforced in the shared agent runtime or as custom gateway logic (most gateways budget per key or team, not per run), so one looping agent cannot exhaust a team's budget. Monitor runs that hit the cap and spend per completed task.","**Scoped tool credentials from the core** — agents get short-lived, least-privilege credentials for each internal system through a shared tool layer instead of team-held service accounts. Monitor tool calls denied by scope and any credential used outside its registered agent."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Every agent team needs model access, tool credentials, trace logging,\n  per-run budgets and an approval step. The platform boundary decides whether you get those from a\n  shared core or build them yourself, and whether your agent can be audited and its model swapped.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The gateway endpoint and per-team key your agent calls, the model\n  alias it requests, the trace schema its steps are logged in, and shared tool servers (for example MCP\n  servers in front of internal systems) with scoped credentials.\n- **What you control.** Calling the platform's stable interfaces instead of a vendor SDK, tagging every\n  request with team and feature, and keeping your agent loop, prompts and tool selection team-owned.\n- **What you can skip.** Funding-model politics and org design detail; know the three scoring questions\n  and the break-even inequality well enough to argue where your agent's needs belong.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Onboarding the second agent team.** The platform gives them a gateway key, a model alias, a trace\n  schema and scoped tool credentials on day one, so they build only their agent loop.\n- **Swapping a model under every agent.** A deprecated model is replaced by remapping an alias once each\n  agent team's evals pass on the replacement.\n- **Containing a runaway agent.** Per-run step and spend caps stop one looping agent before it exhausts\n  its team's monthly gateway budget.\n- **Auditing what an agent did.** One trace store answers which agent run made a CRM change, with which\n  credential, after which tool result.","mini_project":"**A thin agent platform core (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** put a small gateway in front of a tool-calling agent that enforces per-run budgets and logs\n  every step in one trace schema.\n- **Steps:** write a proxy function that every model call goes through, adding team, feature and run-ID\n  tags; cap steps and spend per run; route tool calls through a wrapper that checks a per-agent scope list;\n  write each model call and tool call as one trace record; run a mock agent with two tools, including one\n  forced loop.\n- **Done when:** the looping run is stopped at the cap, an out-of-scope tool call is denied and logged, and\n  a per-feature cost report and a single run's full trajectory can both be printed from the trace store."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Recurring capability from inventory\"] --> X{\"High externality?\"}\n    X -->|\"yes: spend, audit, residency\"| CORE[\"Mandatory core\"]\n    X -->|\"no\"| V{\"Variance between teams?\"}\n    V -->|\"high\"| TEAM[\"Team-owned, revisit later\"]\n    V -->|\"low\"| N{\"Three or more consumers?\"}\n    N -->|\"not yet\"| TEAM\n    N -->|\"yes\"| E{\"Break-even holds?\"}\n    E -->|\"yes\"| PAVED[\"Paved road default\"]\n    E -->|\"no\"| TEAM\n    CORE --> I[\"Stable interface: gateway, tools, traces\"]\n    PAVED --> I","caption":"Run each capability, agent tool credentials included, through this decision path: externalities pull it\ninto the core even at low demand, while high variance (agent loops) keeps it with teams."},"interview":[{"q":"What belongs in a mandatory AI platform core, and why make it mandatory at all?","a":"- **The core:** identity, approved model access through a gateway, budgets and rate limits, common\n  logging and data-classification rules.\n- **Mandatory because its value is universality.** One inventory, one audit trail and one place to swap a\n  deprecated model only work if every workload goes through it; everything else can be an optional paved\n  road."},{"q":"Teams keep bypassing your gateway with their own provider keys. How do you investigate and fix it?","a":"- **Find the reason first:** check the backlog for the top consumer requests, the added latency, missing\n  provider features, and whether chargeback made bypassing cheaper locally.\n- **Fix the cause:** allow pass-through for provider-specific features, cut lead time on top requests,\n  switch to showback if billing drives the bypass.\n- **Then close the door:** block direct provider keys and reconcile invoices against registered workloads."},{"q":"When would you deliberately leave duplication in place?","a":"- **High-variance capabilities** such as retrieval strategy or agent frameworks, because a shared\n  abstraction pushes adoption cost close to each team's own build cost and freezes the wrong design.\n- **Early, low-externality needs** with fewer than three consumers, since the real variation is not yet\n  visible; keep a light standard (trace format) so convergence later is cheap."},{"q":"How do you decide between central funding, showback and chargeback?","a":"- **Start with central funding plus showback**, because adoption matters most while the platform is not\n  yet the default path, and showback builds cost awareness without friction.\n- **Move to chargeback for variable costs** such as tokens once usage is significant and the core is\n  unavoidable; earlier, billing pushes teams to route around the platform."},{"q":"The gateway is on every AI request path. What do you put in its design review?","a":"- **Availability** set by the most demanding consumer, stateless replicas and horizontal scaling.\n- **Failure mode per traffic class:** fail closed for high-risk traffic, fail open to a direct route only\n  where policy allows, decided before the first incident.\n- **Numbers to watch:** added p99 latency, error rate per upstream model, and share of traffic through it."},{"q":"Three agent teams each built their own CRM tool wrapper. What, if anything, do you centralise?","a":"- **Centralise access, not behaviour.** Move credentials, scopes and audit logging of CRM calls into a\n  shared tool server or tool layer, because over-scoped write access is an organisation-wide risk.\n- **Leave the agent side with teams:** which tools each agent sees, how they are described in its prompt,\n  and its loop and planning, since those vary by use case.\n- **Check the economics:** run the break-even with the incident term, and track tool-call errors per team\n  after migration so the shared wrapper does not hurt tool selection."}],"tradeoffs":["**Mandatory core vs latency and autonomy.** One inventory, one audit trail and one place to swap models, but an extra hop and a dependency; it flips toward federation when latency budgets are tight or teams are in separate regulatory domains.","**Wait for the third consumer vs early consistency.** Generalising from real needs tolerates some early duplication; it flips when an externality (data residency, spend) makes even two divergent stacks unacceptable.","**Provider abstraction vs provider features.** Abstracting over providers enables switching and routing, but hides provider-specific features (caching modes, tool formats); allow pass-through for teams that need them.","**Chargeback vs adoption.** Chargeback aligns usage with cost but taxes adoption; prefer showback until the platform is the default path and token spend is material.","**Shared tool servers vs agent-specific tools.** One tool server per internal system removes duplicate wrappers and centralises scopes, but its generic tool descriptions may suit no agent well; let teams wrap or rename tools on top when their eval shows tool-selection errors."],"pitfalls":["**Building a comprehensive platform before any consumer exists.** Symptom: a polished system with low adoption and teams quietly keeping their own API keys. Fix: build from the first projects' shared parts.","**Centralising high-variance capabilities like retrieval strategy.** Teams file endless feature requests or fork the shared component, and its configuration surface balloons.","**A platform team measured on output, not adoption.** It ships features nobody asked for while the top consumer request ages in the backlog.","**Leaking one vendor's SDK through the platform interface.** The first provider migration turns into a change in every consuming service.","**No exit path for teams off the paved road.** Exceptions accumulate as unreviewed shadow stacks that show up as provider invoices finance cannot map to any registered workload.","**Running the gateway like an internal tool rather than tier-one infrastructure.** A single gateway incident takes down every AI feature at once, followed by teams asking for bypass routes.","**Leaving agent tool credentials with each team.** Symptom: service accounts with write scopes nobody remembers granting, and no way to say which agent made a change. Fix: issue scoped credentials from the core and log every tool call against an agent run ID."],"when":"- **Use when:** two or three teams are building on models, or earlier if a compliance or spend externality\n  already exists.\n- **Start with the thin core:** gateway, identity, budgets, logging. Add paved-road components when demand\n  is proven and variance is low. For agents, add scoped tool credentials and trajectory traces to the core\n  as soon as the first agent can write to a system of record.\n- **Measure:** adoption, time to first production call, share of traffic and spend through the core, lead\n  time on the platform's top consumer requests, and the number of approved exceptions (a rising count\n  means the paved road no longer fits).\n- **Avoid:** building a platform as the first AI project; build it out of the first projects' shared parts.\n- **Interviews:** show you can name what to centralise and why, and when you would deliberately leave\n  duplication in place.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Consumes the platform through its stable interface and contributes back components that a second team clearly needs, rather than forking or bypassing it.","architect":"Owns the boundary between mandatory core, paved road and team-owned layers, and the interfaces that keep vendor choices reversible.","fde":"Notices which customer solutions repeat across accounts and argues, with the evidence, for turning them into platform or product capabilities."},"check":{"q":"Two teams want a shared agent framework built by the platform team; four other teams use plain\nmodel calls. Should the platform build it now?","a":"Probably not yet. Two consumers is thin demand, and agent frameworks are high-variance: tool\ncontracts, state handling and orchestration differ a lot between use cases and the ecosystem is still\nmoving. A shared abstraction now would likely encode one team's design and become a constraint for the\nother. What the platform should provide now are the low-variance parts both teams need anyway: model\naccess through the gateway, tracing, budgets, sandboxed tool credentials. Revisit when a third team needs\nagents and the common shape is visible, and keep a light standard (trace format, approval-gate pattern)\nso convergence later is cheap."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which capability is the strongest candidate for the mandatory platform core?","options":["A chunking strategy tuned for one team's contracts","An agent framework used by two teams","Approved model access with per-team budgets and audit logging","A shared prompt library for marketing copy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Model access, budgets and audit are needed by every team, vary little, and create organisation-wide risk when done inconsistently. The chunking strategy and agent framework are high-variance and fit poorly."},{"q":"Why is \"wait for the third consumer\" a common heuristic before platformising a capability?","options":["Platform teams are usually staffed only after three projects exist","By then the real variation in needs is visible, so the shared design generalises from evidence","Licensing for shared tools typically requires three internal customers","Two teams can always share code without a platform"],"answer":1,"explanation":"One or two consumers give too little signal to separate essential needs from incidental ones. The heuristic is overridden when an externality such as data residency makes divergence unacceptable early."},{"q":"What is the usual effect of switching to chargeback before the platform is the default path?","options":["Adoption rises because teams can see their costs","Platform costs fall because usage becomes more efficient","Some teams route around the platform to avoid the bill, recreating shadow stacks","It has no effect, because the costs are the same either way"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Billing teams for a platform they could bypass makes bypassing look cheaper locally. Showback first builds cost awareness without that incentive, and chargeback works once the core is unavoidable."}]},{"key":"strategy/vendor-evaluation","sources":[],"why":"- **AI vendors differ most on what a demo never shows.** The decisive properties are often\n  non-functional: whether your data is retained or used for training, which regions process it, what rate\n  limits you get, how much notice before a model is deprecated, and how hard it is to leave.\n- **Public benchmarks do not transfer reliably.** They measure tasks that may look nothing like yours, and\n  some benchmark data has leaked into training sets, so a leaderboard gap may not transfer.\n- **Demos and prices are not comparable.** Demos run on the vendor's chosen examples; pricing is per token,\n  per query, per seat or per \"credit\", incomparable until converted to cost per unit of your work.\n- **The naive process fails predictably.** A feature checklist rewards breadth over fit; a demo hides\n  failure modes on your messy inputs; the list price ignores that a cheaper model needing two retries,\n  longer prompts or more human review can cost more per correct outcome. Teams discover the gaps after\n  integration, when switching is expensive, and live with a vendor that fails a security review or\n  throttles them at peak.\n- **Evaluation recurs.** Model vendors ship new versions frequently, so the harness should be re-runnable\n  when a new candidate appears, not a one-off procurement exercise. For an agent, a new model version can\n  change tool-calling behaviour even when single-answer quality looks the same.","how":"### 1. Write requirements as gates and weighted criteria\n- **Must-haves are pass/fail gates:** data use (no training on your data, retention period, zero-retention\n  options), data residency, security attestations and the answers your security questionnaire needs,\n  required deployment mode (API, VPC, self-hosted), and compliance obligations.\n- **A vendor that fails a gate is out regardless of score**, *because* a weighted average lets a high\n  quality score compensate for a disqualifying flaw.\n- **Differentiators are scored**, with weights fixed **before** seeing results, so the scoring is not\n  reverse-engineered to justify a favourite.\n\n### 2. Build a task-specific evaluation set\n- **A few hundred real, representative, labelled examples** from your workflow, including the hard and ugly\n  cases (scanned PDFs, long threads, other languages).\n- **Keep it private.** Never send it to vendors for \"tuning\" before the test, *because* then you are\n  measuring their preparation, not their product.\n- **Size it for the differences you need to detect.** The 95% interval on accuracy *p* measured on *n*\n  items is roughly ±1.96 × √(p(1−p)/n): at p = 0.95 and n = 400 that is about ±2 points. Two vendors 1\n  point apart on 400 items are tied, and the decision must rest on cost, latency or terms.\n- **Compare *paired*.** Every vendor runs on the same items, so count items A gets right and B gets wrong,\n  and vice versa. Only the disagreements carry information, which makes paired comparison more sensitive\n  than comparing two headline percentages; reading them tells you *what kind* of input each vendor fails\n  on.\n\n### 3. Run a controlled bake-off\n1. **Same harness, same scoring, and an equal prompt-engineering budget per vendor**, tuned on a separate\n   development split and scored once on the held-out test split.\n2. **Do not reuse prompts tuned for the incumbent**, *because* they encode that model's quirks and quietly\n   favour it.\n3. **If outputs are sampled**, run each item more than once or fix the decoding settings, and report the\n   spread.\n4. **Measure for each candidate:**\n\n| Metric | How |\n|---|---|\n| **Quality** | Your metric: accuracy, faithfulness, pass rate on acceptance criteria. |\n| **Latency** | p50 and p95 at your expected concurrency, not single requests, *because* rate limits and queueing appear only under load. |\n| **Cost per successful task** | Total cost (tokens, retries, fixed fees, human review of failures) divided by tasks completed correctly. The number that makes different pricing models comparable. |\n| **Operational fit** | Quotas in your region, SLA terms, observability hooks, SDK quality, support. |\n\n- **Count each cost once.** If a human fixes a failure, that task is delivered and its review cost is in\n  the numerator. If failures are simply discarded, there is no review cost but they drop out of the\n  denominator.\n- **Non-model vendors use the same structure with different metrics.** A vector database is measured on\n  recall@k and p95 query latency at your corpus size and with your metadata filters, plus ingestion and\n  update throughput, *because* index behaviour changes with scale and filter selectivity in ways a small\n  demo corpus never shows.\n\n### Agent-specific bake-off metrics\nWhen the model drives an agent loop, score the whole run, not one call:\n\n| Metric | Why it matters for an agent |\n|---|---|\n| **Task-completion rate** | The end state is checked (ticket routed, record updated), because a run can make plausible calls and still fail. |\n| **Tool-call validity** | Share of tool calls with valid arguments for the schema; invalid calls cost a retry step each. |\n| **Steps per completed task** | More steps mean more tokens, more latency and more chances to go wrong. |\n| **End-to-end latency** | Per-step latency times steps, measured at load, because users wait for the whole run. |\n| **Cost per completed task** | All steps, retries and review of failed runs, divided by runs that completed correctly. |\n\n- **Run each task more than once** when the loop samples, because one lucky trajectory hides a flaky\n  agent; report the spread.\n\n### 4. Assess the vendor, not just the product\n- **Stability:** financial and roadmap stability.\n- **Change management:** model deprecation policy and notice period, how changes are communicated, and\n  whether a model version can be pinned.\n- **Small vendors:** ask what happens to your data and service if they are acquired or shut down.\n\n### 5. Price the exit before you enter\n- **Estimate the cost of leaving:** proprietary formats, embeddings that must be regenerated, prompts tuned\n  to one model, contractual minimums.\n- **Prefer vendors that fit behind your own interface** (gateway, standard APIs), *because* a low exit\n  cost is what keeps the next negotiation honest.\n\n### 6. Decide, document, and schedule re-evaluation\n- **Decision record:** the scores, the gates and the reasoning.\n- **Re-run the harness** when a material new option or price change appears.","example":"### Scoring three providers for invoice field extraction\nQuality and latency come from a bake-off on 400 labelled invoices; prices and figures are illustrative.\n\n```python\nvendors = {\n    #       gates_ok, accuracy, p95_s, cost_per_call, review_cost_per_failure\n    \"A\": (True,  0.96, 2.8, 0.010, 0.50),\n    \"B\": (True,  0.91, 1.2, 0.002, 0.50),\n    \"C\": (False, 0.97, 1.9, 0.006, 0.50),   # fails residency gate: data leaves the region\n}\nweights = {\"cost\": 0.5, \"latency\": 0.2, \"accuracy\": 0.3}   # fixed before the bake-off\nP95_BUDGET = 3.0\n\ndef cost_per_success(acc, call, review):\n    # every invoice ends correct: the model's output, or a human fix of a flagged failure\n    return call + (1 - acc) * review   # each pays one call; failures also pay a review\n\nrows = {}\nfor v, (ok, acc, p95, call, review) in vendors.items():\n    if not ok or p95 > P95_BUDGET:\n        print(v, \"excluded by gate\"); continue\n    rows[v] = (acc, p95, cost_per_success(acc, call, review))\n\nbest_cost = min(r[2] for r in rows.values()); best_p95 = min(r[1] for r in rows.values())\nfor v, (acc, p95, cps) in rows.items():\n    score = (weights[\"cost\"] * best_cost / cps + weights[\"latency\"] * best_p95 / p95\n             + weights[\"accuracy\"] * acc)\n    print(f\"{v}: acc={acc:.2f} p95={p95}s cost/success=${cps:.4f} score={score:.3f}\")\n# C excluded by gate\n# A: acc=0.96 p95=2.8s cost/success=$0.0300 score=0.874\n# B: acc=0.91 p95=1.2s cost/success=$0.0470 score=0.792\n```\n\n- **The cheap call is the expensive outcome.** B's call price is five times lower, but its extra 5 points\n  of errors each need a 50-cent human fix, so its cost per successful extraction is higher than A's\n  ($0.047 vs $0.030).\n- **C is out anyway.** It has the best accuracy, but its one-point lead over A is inside the ±2-point noise\n  on 400 items, so it was not a clear winner even before the gate. A's 5-point lead over B is outside it.\n- **The model assumes failures are caught** (by validation rules or review). If they slip through\n  undetected, their downstream cost belongs in the review term and usually dwarfs the call price.\n- **Accuracy enters twice**, inside cost per success and as its own weighted term. That is defensible only\n  if the accuracy weight stands for something cost does not capture (undetected errors, user trust);\n  otherwise drop it, or the scoring double-counts.\n\n### Is the gap real? A paired comparison on the same 400 items\nCounts of items only one vendor got right are illustrative but consistent with the accuracies above.\n\n```python\nfrom math import comb, sqrt\n\ndef ci95(p, n):\n    return 1.96 * sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)        # normal approximation to the 95% interval\n\ndef paired_p(only_a, only_b):\n    # exact two-sided sign test on the disagreements (McNemar's exact test)\n    k, m = min(only_a, only_b), only_a + only_b\n    tail = sum(comb(m, i) for i in range(k + 1)) / 2 ** m\n    return min(1.0, 2 * tail)\n\nn = 400\nprint(f\"+/- {ci95(0.95, n):.3f} at p=0.95, n={n}\")\nfor name, only_x, only_y in [(\"A vs B\", 26, 6), (\"C vs A\", 10, 6)]:\n    gap = (only_x - only_y) / n\n    print(f\"{name}: gap {gap:+.3f}, disagreements {only_x + only_y}, p = {paired_p(only_x, only_y):.4f}\")\n# +/- 0.021 at p=0.95, n=400\n# A vs B: gap +0.050, disagreements 32, p = 0.0005\n# C vs A: gap +0.010, disagreements 16, p = 0.4545\n```\n\n| Pair | Only first right | Only second right | Gap | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A vs B | 26 | 6 | 5 points | Real: decide on it |\n| C vs A | 10 | 6 | 1 point | Tied: decide on cost, latency, terms |\n\n- **Read the 32 and 16 disagreeing items by hand**; they show which input types (scans, other languages)\n  each vendor fails on.\n\n### Worked scenario: two models behind a support-ticket agent\nEach candidate runs the same agent loop (same tools, tool descriptions tuned per model on a dev split) on\n200 held-out tickets. A failed run goes to a human at $0.50 per ticket. All figures are illustrative.\n\n| Measure | Model A | Model B |\n|---|---|---|\n| Price per step (tokens in and out) | $0.012 | $0.004 |\n| Average steps per run | 7 | 11 |\n| Cost per run | 7 × 0.012 = $0.084 | 11 × 0.004 = $0.044 |\n| Task-completion rate | 0.86 | 0.74 |\n| Cost per ticket incl. human fix of failures | 0.084 + 0.14 × 0.50 = $0.154 | 0.044 + 0.26 × 0.50 = $0.174 |\n| p95 per step at load | 1.4 s | 0.9 s |\n| Rough run time (p95 per step × average steps) | ~9.8 s | ~9.9 s |\n\n- **B is three times cheaper per step and still loses.** It needs more steps (weaker tool selection) and\n  fails more runs, so the human fixes dominate.\n- **Faster steps did not buy a faster agent**, because B takes more of them.\n- **Check the gap against noise:** on 200 runs the interval on 0.86 alone is about ±5 points, and on the\n  difference of two independent rates about ±8, so the 12-point completion gap is real (a paired count on\n  the same tickets is tighter still); read the failed trajectories to see where B goes wrong.","patterns":["**Re-runnable bake-off harness** — keep the held-out set, each vendor's prompts (tuned on the dev split) and the scorer in one versioned job that emits the quality, p95 and cost-per-success table. Re-run it when a vendor ships a model or changes price; monitor deltas against the current choice.","**Load test at production concurrency** — drive each candidate with a load tool such as Locust or k6 at expected peak concurrency, within the quota you will actually be granted in your region. Record p50, p95 and the rate of HTTP 429 (rate-limited) responses, not just averages.","**Weights and gates in the decision record before results** — commit the gates, weights and latency budget (like `P95_BUDGET` above) to the record before the bake-off runs. Monitor whether later audits or new stakeholders can reproduce the decision from it.","**Pinned versions and a deprecation calendar** — configure a dated model version rather than a floating alias in the gateway, and log each vendor's deprecation notices with a re-validation date well before retirement. Monitor days of notice remaining per pinned model.","**Exit-cost line in every contract review** — record proprietary formats, embedding regeneration cost and contractual minimums alongside price, and prefer short terms in fast-moving categories. Monitor the estimate at each renewal.","**Trajectory-level agent bake-off** — replay the same held-out agent tasks against each candidate model through your own loop and mock or sandboxed tools, scoring the end state of each run. Monitor completion rate, invalid tool calls, steps per completed task and cost per completed task per model version.","**Shadow runs before switching an agent's model** — send a sample of live agent tasks to the challenger in read-only mode, with tool writes disabled, and compare its proposed actions with the incumbent's. Monitor the action-disagreement rate before any cutover."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent multiplies every vendor property by its step count: a model\n  that is slightly worse at tool calls fails more runs, latency adds up across steps, and a cheap per-call\n  price can become an expensive completed task once extra steps and retries are counted.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Choosing the model behind your agent loop, the vector store behind\n  its retrieval tool, and eval or tracing tools; each choice sits behind your gateway or tool interface.\n- **What you control.** The agent eval set (real tasks with checkable outcomes), an equal prompt and\n  tool-description budget per candidate, measuring at production concurrency, and the cost-per-completed-\n  task formula.\n- **What you can skip.** Procurement mechanics and contract negotiation detail; know the gates, the noise\n  interval on your eval size and the exit-cost questions.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Picking the model for a new agent.** Two or three providers run the same agent loop on real tasks,\n  and cost per completed task decides between those that pass the gates.\n- **Re-validating after a model release.** The bake-off job re-runs the agent eval when a provider ships a\n  new version, catching tool-calling regressions before the alias is remapped.\n- **Choosing the vector store behind a retrieval tool.** Recall@k and p95 at your corpus size and filters\n  decide, not a demo corpus.\n- **Deciding on a hosted agent platform.** Gates on data use and trace ownership, then exit cost, before\n  any feature comparison.","mini_project":"**Agent model bake-off (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** compare two models as the brain of the same small tool-calling agent on your own tasks.\n- **Steps:** write 40 tasks with checkable end states and two mock tools; split 10 for prompt tuning and 30\n  held out; run each model three times per held-out task through the same loop; log completion, invalid tool\n  calls, steps, tokens, latency per step and cost; compute cost per completed task with a review cost for\n  failed runs; compute the noise interval on completion.\n- **Done when:** you have one table per model, a paired count of tasks only one model completed, and a\n  written recommendation that says whether the gap is outside the noise and what drove the cost."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Candidate vendors\"] --> G{\"Pass every gate?\"}\n    G -->|\"no: data use, residency, security\"| X[\"Excluded\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| T[\"Tune prompts on dev split\"]\n    T --> B[\"Bake-off on held-out set at load\"]\n    B --> M[\"Completion, p95, cost per task\"]\n    M --> P{\"Paired gap outside noise?\"}\n    P -->|\"yes\"| S[\"Weighted score, fixed weights\"]\n    P -->|\"no\"| CT[\"Decide on cost, latency, terms\"]\n    S --> D[\"Decision record and exit cost\"]\n    CT --> D\n    D -.->|\"new model or price change\"| B","caption":"Gates remove candidates before any scoring; only survivors run your agent tasks in the bake-off, and a\ngap inside the noise sends the decision to cost, latency and terms."},"interview":[{"q":"Why not just pick the model that tops the public leaderboard?","a":"- **Leaderboards measure someone else's task.** Benchmarks may look nothing like your inputs, and some\n  benchmark data has leaked into training sets, so a leaderboard gap may not transfer.\n- **The decisive properties are elsewhere:** data terms, residency, rate limits, deprecation notice and\n  exit cost, plus quality on your own messy inputs, which only your own labelled set measures."},{"q":"How large does your evaluation set need to be to choose between two vendors?","a":"- **Work from the interval:** ±1.96 × √(p(1−p)/n) is about ±2 points at 95% accuracy on 400 items, so a\n  1-point gap at that size is a tie.\n- **Use paired comparison** on the same items: only the disagreements carry information, so a sign test on\n  them is more sensitive than comparing two headline percentages.\n- **If you need to detect smaller gaps,** label more items or let cost, latency and terms decide."},{"q":"The incumbent vendor wins every bake-off you run. What might be wrong?","a":"- **Prompt bias:** challengers are run on prompts tuned for the incumbent, which encode its quirks.\n- **Fix the protocol:** an equal prompt-engineering budget per vendor on a development split, a single\n  scoring run on the held-out split, and fixed decoding settings or repeated runs for sampled outputs.\n- **Check the set** too: if it was built from cases the incumbent already handles, it is biased toward it."},{"q":"When is a cheaper, less accurate model the right choice for an agent step?","a":"- **When errors are cheap to catch.** If validation rules flag failures and a fix costs little, cost per\n  successful task can favour the cheaper model despite lower accuracy.\n- **Not when failures slip through undetected,** because their downstream cost belongs in the review term\n  and usually dwarfs the call price; compute cost per success both ways before deciding.\n- **In an agent, count steps too:** a cheaper model that needs more steps or fails more runs can cost more\n  per completed task, so it often fits narrow sub-steps (routing, extraction) better than the main loop."},{"q":"How would you evaluate vector database vendors differently from model providers?","a":"- **Same structure, different metrics:** gates first, then a bake-off on recall@k and p95 query latency at\n  your corpus size and with your metadata filters, plus ingestion and update throughput.\n- **Test at real scale and filter selectivity,** because index behaviour changes with both in ways a small\n  demo corpus never shows; then price exit, since embeddings may need regenerating."},{"q":"How would you choose the model behind a production tool-calling agent?","a":"- **Gates first,** then run each candidate through your own agent loop on held-out real tasks with the same\n  tools, tuning prompts and tool descriptions per model on a dev split.\n- **Score the run, not the call:** task-completion rate, invalid tool calls, steps per completed task,\n  end-to-end latency at load and cost per completed task including human fixes of failed runs.\n- **Before cutover,** shadow the challenger on live tasks with writes disabled and compare its proposed\n  actions with the incumbent's."}],"tradeoffs":["**Rigorous bake-off vs speed.** Decisions transfer to production, but cost weeks of labelling and engineering; for low-risk, easily reversible choices a lighter pilot is enough.","**Multi-vendor vs simplicity.** Several vendors behind a gateway reduce lock-in and outage risk, but multiply security reviews, contracts and prompt maintenance; they pay off when volume or criticality is high.","**Top quality vs price.** The top-quality vendor maximises accuracy but may cost more per call; it flips when error costs are low and a cheaper model's failures are cheap to catch.","**Commitment discounts vs exit cost.** Long-term commitments buy discounts, but raise exit cost in a market where prices often fall and better models appear; keep terms short when the category is moving fast.","**Agent framework or platform vendor vs your own loop.** A hosted agent platform ships tools, memory and tracing quickly, but owns your loop and traces and raises exit cost; it flips toward your own thin loop once the agent is core to the product."],"pitfalls":["**Trusting public leaderboards for a domain task.** Symptom: a vendor that ranked first loses on your own invoices or tickets. Fix: your own labelled set.","**Comparing list prices instead of cost per successful task.** The cheap option turns out expensive once retries and human correction are counted.","**Testing latency with single requests.** Production hits rate limits or queueing at peak and p95 latency breaks the user-facing budget.","**Setting weights after seeing results.** The decision record reads like a justification, and the next audit or new stakeholder challenges it.","**Ignoring deprecation policy.** A pinned model is retired with short notice and the team must re-validate prompts and evals in a hurry.","**Running challengers on prompts tuned for the incumbent.** The incumbent wins every bake-off, and the gap shrinks or vanishes once someone spends a few days prompting the challenger properly.","**Declaring a winner on a one- or two-point gap from a few hundred items.** The \"better\" vendor's lead evaporates on the next re-run or the production sample.","**Comparing agent models on single-turn accuracy.** The winner on one-shot answers loops, calls tools with bad arguments or stops early inside the agent. Fix: score full trajectories on real tasks and count cost per completed task."],"when":"- **Use the full process when** the choice is expensive to reverse or touches sensitive data: primary\n  model provider, vector store, platform vendors, anything inside the customer's data boundary.\n- **Use a light version** (gates plus a small bake-off) for tools that are cheap to swap.\n- **For agents, always run the bake-off through the loop:** completion rate, steps and cost per completed\n  task on real tasks, before switching the model behind a production agent.\n- **Measure:** quality on your own set with its confidence interval, p95 latency at load, cost per\n  successful task, and exit cost. Re-run when new models ship or prices change.\n- **Interviews:** the signal is separating gates from scores, insisting on your own data, and converting\n  pricing to cost per correct outcome.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the reusable bake-off harness, runs candidates on the same labelled set, and reports quality, p95 latency and cost per success in one table.","architect":"Defines the gates and weights, owns the decision record, and makes sure the chosen vendor sits behind an interface that keeps exit cost low.","fde":"Evaluates vendors against the customer's own constraints (their region, procurement rules, security questionnaire) and data, not the vendor's demo set."},"check":{"q":"Vendor X's model is cheaper per token than Vendor Y's and scores slightly lower on your eval.\nProcurement wants X. What do you check before agreeing?","a":"First, that X passes every gate: data use and retention terms, residency, security answers,\ndeployment mode. Next, whether \"slightly lower\" is real: on a few hundred items a one- or two-point gap\nmay be noise, so I would look at the paired disagreements and what kind of inputs X gets wrong. Then convert both to cost per successful task: X may need longer prompts, more retries or\nmore human correction, and if failures cost real review time, a small accuracy gap can outweigh a\nlarge token discount. I would also check p95 latency and rate limits at our concurrency, the deprecation\npolicy and version pinning, and the exit cost. If X still wins on cost per success within the latency\nbudget, X is the right call; if not, the table makes the case to procurement in their own terms."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are must-have requirements handled as gates instead of heavily weighted criteria?","options":["Gates are faster to evaluate than weighted scores","A weighted average lets strong scores elsewhere compensate for a disqualifying flaw","Vendors prefer pass/fail requirements in procurement","Weighted scoring cannot handle more than five criteria"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A vendor that violates residency or trains on your data is unacceptable whatever its accuracy. Any finite weight lets enough quality offset the violation, so the requirement must be a hard filter."},{"q":"Which metric best compares vendors that use different pricing models?","options":["Price per million input tokens","Monthly platform fee","Total cost divided by the number of tasks completed correctly","Rank on a public benchmark divided by price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cost per successful task folds in token prices, retries, fixed fees and the cost of handling failures, so a per-token discount and a per-seat plan become directly comparable on the outcome you pay for."},{"q":"Why should latency in a bake-off be measured at expected concurrency?","options":["Single-request latency is always higher than under load","Vendors charge less for concurrent requests","Rate limits and queueing only appear under load, and they drive p95 latency","Concurrency improves accuracy through batching"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One request at a time hides throttling and queueing delays. The user-facing latency budget is usually a tail percentile, so it must be measured under production-like concurrency."}]},{"key":"strategy/technical-due-diligence","sources":[],"why":"- **Deals are priced on claims:** \"proprietary model\", \"95% accuracy\", \"data moat\", \"70% gross margin\",\n  \"enterprise-ready\".\n- **AI adds failure modes a code review cannot see.** Conventional diligence (code quality, architecture,\n  security, team) still matters, but:\n  - accuracy can be an artefact of a test set the target curated or trained on;\n  - a \"proprietary model\" may be prompts around a third-party API whose terms or prices can change;\n  - training data may lack the rights the business model assumes;\n  - inference costs scale with usage, so margins healthy at current volume can compress as customers use\n    the product more, or as it adds longer context or agentic loops.\n- **The naive approach checks presentation, not reality.** Slides, a demo, the architecture diagram and\n  founder answers: a demo runs on chosen inputs, a diagram shows intent rather than what is deployed, and\n  the people answering have every reason to be optimistic.\n- **The cost of a miss is asymmetric.** Discovering after closing that accuracy does not hold on the\n  acquirer's customers, or that a key dataset cannot be used commercially, can erase the deal's value.\n  Diligence turns claims into evidence first.","how":"### The six diligence questions\nFor AI, diligence centres on six questions; steps 1-8 below work through them, and the agent-specific\nchecks follow:\n\n| # | Question |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Does the AI work on data the target did not choose? |\n| 2 | Who owns its data and models? |\n| 3 | What happens to margins as usage grows? |\n| 4 | How exposed is it to upstream model providers? |\n| 5 | Is the AI layer secure? |\n| 6 | Can the system and team be operated by someone else? |\n\n### 1. Start from the thesis, not a checklist\n- **Write down the claims the deal depends on:** \"their extraction is materially better than what we could\n  build in a year\", \"margins hold at 10× volume\", \"we can deploy it in our regulated customers' VPCs\".\n- **Each claim becomes a hypothesis** with the evidence that would confirm or refute it.\n- **Why:** it focuses limited time, *because* a generic checklist spends equal effort on things that do not\n  affect value.\n\n### 2. Verify the AI actually works, independently\n1. **Request** their eval methodology, datasets and recent results.\n2. **Run the system on a held-out sample *you* provide**, from your own or your customers' data, scored by\n   your criteria.\n3. **Ask how the eval set was built** and whether any of it was used in training or prompt tuning\n   (contamination).\n4. **Treat the gap** between their number and yours as the most informative finding in AI diligence.\n5. **Explain the gap**, *because* its cause decides the remedy: break both results down by input segment\n   (document type, language, scan quality, customer).\n\n| Pattern in the segments | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Equally good per segment, only the mix differs | The overall number reflects the target's own customer base; predict yours by re-weighting per-segment accuracy by your mix. |\n| Worse on the same segments | The reported number was inflated by curation or tuning on the eval set. |\n\n- **Plan the data mechanics early**, because deals rarely allow free data flow: the target runs its system\n  on your sample under NDA while you hold the labels and do the scoring, or the test runs in a clean room\n  with a small \"clean team\" who do not share raw data with the wider deal team.\n\n### 3. Map dependencies and their terms\n- **List every model, API, dataset and open-source component** in the production path, with license and\n  contract terms.\n- **Ask:** is a third-party model's output allowed in this use; can the provider change pricing or\n  deprecate the model; do open-weight model licenses permit the intended commercial scale; is data\n  processed in regions customers accept?\n- **Stress question:** what share of the product would survive the main provider doubling prices or\n  withdrawing a model?\n\n### 4. Establish data rights and provenance\n- **For training, fine-tuning and retrieval data:** where it came from, under what consent or license,\n  whether customer data is used across customers, and whether deletion requests can be honoured, including\n  in trained weights.\n- **Missing provenance** is a legal and reputational liability that transfers with the deal.\n\n### 5. Rebuild the unit economics\n- **From billing data and traces,** compute cost per request and per customer.\n- **Project at higher usage:** token growth per task, retries, agent steps, human review.\n- **Check pass-through:** does the target's pricing model pass those costs on?\n- **Margins that depend on a provider's current discount are a risk, not a fact.**\n\n### 6. Check security of the AI layer\nBeyond the usual application review, ask the AI-specific questions:\n- **Tenant scoping:** is retrieval scoped per tenant, so one customer's documents can never appear in\n  another's context, including through shared caches or indexes?\n- **Agent blast radius:** what can an agentic feature do with its tool credentials if a document contains\n  a prompt injection?\n- **Logging:** where do prompts and outputs get logged, and for how long?\n- **Supply chain:** where do model weights and datasets come from, and are third-party checkpoints loaded in\n  formats that can execute code?\n- **Why:** a cross-tenant leak in an acquired product becomes the acquirer's incident.\n\n### 7. Check operability and the team\n- **Operations:** monitoring, incident history, an eval-gated release process, reproducible training.\n- **Rebuildability:** can the system be rebuilt from the repository?\n- **Key-person risk:** how concentrated is knowledge?\n- **Interview engineers separately from leadership**, *because* the most useful information is often the\n  mismatch.\n\n### 8. Classify findings by consequence\n| Class | Response |\n|---|---|\n| **Deal-breaker** | Walk away. |\n| **Price or terms adjustment** | Escrow, warranty, earn-out tied to a metric. |\n| **Post-close remediation** | Budgeted item with an estimated cost. |\n\nThis converts technical findings into decisions the deal team can act on.\n\n### 9. Agent-specific checks\nWhen the product is an agent, add these tests to steps 2, 5 and 6:\n\n| Claim to test | Test | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Completion or \"resolution\" rate | Replay your real tasks in a sandbox and check the end state, not the final message | An agent can reply confidently and leave the task undone |\n| Autonomy level | Read traces for how often a human approved, edited or redid the agent's actions | \"Autonomous\" may hide a human review queue in the cost base |\n| Cost per task | Steps and tokens per completed task on your tasks versus the target's traces | Harder inputs mean more steps, and cost scales with them |\n| Blast radius | List each tool credential's scopes and what one injected instruction could do with them | Over-scoped write access transfers with the deal |\n| Reproducibility | Re-run a sample of logged trajectories and compare outcomes | Unpinned models or prompts make reported results unrepeatable |","example":"### Worked scenario: acquiring an AI contract-review startup\nClaims: \"94% clause-extraction accuracy\" and \"68% gross margin\". All figures are illustrative.\n\n**Accuracy**\n\n| Step | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Target's eval set | 1,000 contracts it selected; 300 of them were also used to iterate on prompts |\n| Acquirer's sample | 200 contracts from its own customers |\n| Result on acquirer's sample | 86%; the 95% interval at 200 contracts is about ±5 points, so the gap to 94% is real, not sampling noise |\n| Clean, digital English contracts (128) | about 95% |\n| Scanned or non-English contracts (72) | about 70%, with an interval of roughly ±10 points at that size |\n| Acquirer's mix 64/36 | 0.64 × 95% + 0.36 × 70% ≈ 86% |\n| Startup's set, about 95% clean | 0.95 × 95% + 0.05 × 70% ≈ 94% |\n\n- **The headline is mostly a mix effect.** The product is genuinely strong on clean contracts and weak on\n  the inputs the acquirer's customers send more often.\n- **The 94% is still optimistic**, because of the prompt tuning on 300 eval contracts.\n- **The actionable finding** is the OCR and multilingual gap, which has a cost to fix that can be\n  estimated.\n\n**Economics**\n- **Today:** revenue per customer averages $4,000 per month; inference costs $900 per month per customer,\n  plus $400 of hosting and support, giving (4,000 − 1,300) / 4,000 ≈ 68%.\n- **Roadmap:** a multi-step agent review that the startup's own traces show uses roughly 3× the tokens per\n  contract. At the same price and token rates, inference becomes $2,700 and margin falls to\n  (4,000 − 3,100) / 4,000 = 22.5%.\n- **Discount risk:** margin also depends on a discounted rate with one provider that expires next year.\n\n**Dependencies:** everything runs on one external model API; no abstraction layer; prompts tuned\nspecifically to that model.\n\n**Classification:** not a deal-breaker.\n- Price adjustment for the margin risk.\n- An earn-out tied to accuracy on the acquirer's own held-out set.\n- Post-close remediation budget for OCR improvements and a gateway abstraction, sized from the 70% segment.\n- Without independent testing, the deal would have been priced on 94% and 68%.\n\n### Reproducing the numbers: mix re-weighting and margin sensitivity\nThe same arithmetic as code, so the deal team can change the mix or token multiplier themselves. The\n+25% rate rise after the discount lapses is an illustrative assumption, not a figure from the scenario.\n\n```python\nfrom math import sqrt\n\ndef ci95(p, n):\n    return 1.96 * sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)              # normal approximation\n\nseg_acc = {\"clean\": 0.95, \"scanned_or_non_english\": 0.70}   # measured on the acquirer's 200\nmixes = {\n    \"startup eval set\":   {\"clean\": 0.95, \"scanned_or_non_english\": 0.05},\n    \"acquirer customers\": {\"clean\": 0.64, \"scanned_or_non_english\": 0.36},\n}\nfor name, mix in mixes.items():\n    print(f\"{name:18} expected accuracy {sum(mix[s] * seg_acc[s] for s in mix):.1%}\")\nprint(f\"interval: +/-{ci95(0.86, 200):.3f} on 200, +/-{ci95(0.70, 72):.3f} on 72\")\n\ndef gross_margin(revenue, inference, other):\n    return (revenue - inference - other) / revenue\n\nprint(f\"today          {gross_margin(4000, 900, 400):.1%}\")\nprint(f\"3x tokens      {gross_margin(4000, 900 * 3, 400):.1%}\")\nprint(f\"3x, +25% rates {gross_margin(4000, 900 * 3 * 1.25, 400):.1%}\")   # illustrative discount lapse\n# startup eval set   expected accuracy 93.8%\n# acquirer customers expected accuracy 86.0%\n# interval: +/-0.048 on 200, +/-0.106 on 72\n# today          67.5%\n# 3x tokens      22.5%\n# 3x, +25% rates 5.6%\n```\n\n- **The re-weighted 93.8% matches the claimed 94%,** which supports the mix explanation over pure\n  inflation.\n- **Two compounding risks** (more tokens per task, a lapsed discount) take margin from about 68% to single\n  digits unless pricing passes costs through.\n\n### Worked scenario: testing a support agent's \"80% resolved\" claim\nThe target sells a ticket-resolution agent. The acquirer replays 150 of its own tickets in a sandbox with\nmock CRM and email tools. All figures are illustrative.\n\n| Step | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Target's definition of resolved | The agent sent a final reply; no check that the ticket's end state was correct |\n| End state checked on 150 tickets | 62% completed; the interval at 150 is about ±8 points, so the gap to 80% is real |\n| Steps per ticket | about 9 on the acquirer's tickets versus about 5 in the target's traces, so at similar tokens per step cost per ticket is roughly 1.8× |\n| Human involvement | traces show a review queue where staff edit a share of replies before sending |\n| Tool scopes | the CRM credential can write to every customer account, not only the ticket's |\n\n- **Classification:** not a deal-breaker if the thesis still holds at 62%; a price adjustment for the\n  completion and cost gap, and a remediation item to scope tool credentials per ticket.\n- **The definition was the finding.** Agree \"completed\" as a checked end state before comparing numbers.","patterns":["**Clean-room independent eval** — when data cannot flow freely, have the target run its system on your sample under NDA while you hold labels and score, or use a clean team. Report accuracy per segment with its interval, not just the headline.","**Trace and billing reconciliation** — to verify architecture claims such as \"proprietary model\", match provider invoices against production traces: which models are called, by which features, at what cost per request. Unexplained provider spend or calls contradict the diagram.","**Usage-sensitive margin model** — build the margin from tokens per task, retries, agent steps, human review and discount expiry dates, then run scenarios such as 10× volume and the roadmap's agentic features. Flag any margin that holds only at today's discounted rates.","**Dependency and license register** — list every model, dataset and open-source component with license, permitted use and region. For third-party checkpoints, check the format: pickle-based files can execute code on load, while safetensors files store tensors only.","**Findings mapped to deal instruments** — each finding carries a class and a remedy: walk away, a price change, escrow or warranty, an earn-out tied to accuracy on your own held-out set, or a costed remediation item. Monitor that every finding has an owner on the deal team.","**Sandbox trajectory replay for agent claims** — run the target's agent on your tasks against mock or sandboxed tools, check end states, and compare steps and tokens per task with its production traces. Report completion with its interval and the share of runs that needed a human.","**Tool-scope audit** — export every credential the agent uses and its scopes, and walk through what a prompt-injected document could make it do. Record each over-scoped credential as a costed remediation."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent products are sold on completion rates, autonomy and margins that\n  are easy to overstate: \"resolved\" may mean the agent replied, not that the task was done, and agent\n  steps multiply inference cost. You may be the engineer asked to test such a claim, or the team whose\n  agent is being tested.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Your traces, eval sets, tool credentials and billing data are the\n  evidence diligence asks for; a sandbox replay of real tasks is the core test.\n- **What you control.** Keeping agent evals on held-out data, end-state checks, scoped tool credentials\n  and trace-to-invoice reconciliation, so your own agent would pass the same scrutiny.\n- **What you can skip.** Deal instruments (escrow, warranties, earn-out mechanics) and legal process;\n  recognise the three finding classes and leave the rest to the deal team.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Acquiring an agent startup.** The acquirer replays its own tasks through the target's agent and\n  prices the gap between claimed and checked completion.\n- **Choosing a critical agent partner.** A lighter diligence pass tests the partner's tool scopes, trace\n  retention and cost per task before its agent touches your systems.\n- **Preparing your own agent for scrutiny.** An agent team keeps held-out evals, end-state checks and\n  trace-to-invoice reconciliation ready for an investor or enterprise buyer.\n- **Stress-testing an agentic roadmap's margin.** Finance asks what a multi-step agent feature does to\n  gross margin, and traces supply tokens per task.","mini_project":"**Diligence pack for a small agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** test a tool-calling agent's headline claim the way an acquirer would.\n- **Steps:** take any small agent (your own or an open-source example) and write its claim as a number;\n  build 30 held-out tasks with checkable end states and mock tools; replay them and log completion, steps,\n  tokens and human-needed runs; list every tool credential and its scope; compute cost per completed task\n  and the margin at an assumed price per task, today and at 3× tokens.\n- **Done when:** you have the claim versus your measured number with its interval, a scope table with any\n  over-scoped tool flagged, and a findings list where each item is a deal-breaker, adjustment or\n  remediation."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Deal thesis: headline claims\"] --> H[\"Claims as testable hypotheses\"]\n    H --> E[\"Sandbox replay of your tasks\"]\n    H --> DM[\"Dependency and license map\"]\n    H --> DR[\"Data rights and provenance\"]\n    H --> UE[\"Usage-sensitive margin model\"]\n    H --> SEC[\"AI-layer security and operability\"]\n    E --> F{\"Classify each finding\"}\n    DM --> F\n    DR --> F\n    UE --> F\n    SEC --> F\n    F -->|\"fatal\"| DB[\"Deal-breaker\"]\n    F -->|\"priced risk\"| ADJ[\"Price, escrow or earn-out\"]\n    F -->|\"fixable\"| REM[\"Post-close remediation budget\"]","caption":"Each headline claim becomes a hypothesis tested by its own evidence workstream (for an agent, a sandbox\nreplay of your tasks); every finding ends in one of three classes the deal team can act on."},"interview":[{"q":"What makes technical due diligence on an AI company different from ordinary software diligence?","a":"- **The claims are statistical and economic,** not just structural: accuracy can be an artefact of a\n  curated or contaminated eval set, and inference cost scales with usage, so margins move as the product\n  grows.\n- **So you test, not read:** an independent eval on data the target did not choose, a dependency and\n  data-rights map, and a usage-sensitive margin model, on top of the usual code and security review."},{"q":"The target reports 94% accuracy and you measure 86% on your sample. How do you explain the gap?","a":"- **Check noise first:** at 200 items the interval on 86% is about ±5 points, so an 8-point gap is real.\n- **Segment both results** by document type, language and scan quality. If per-segment accuracy matches\n  and only the mix differs, re-weight by your mix to predict your number.\n- **If it is worse on the same segments,** the reported number was inflated by curation or tuning on the\n  eval set, which is a different and more serious finding."},{"q":"The target's product depends entirely on one third-party model API. Is that a red flag?","a":"- **Not by itself.** Judge the cost of switching, not the dependency: a target whose real asset is\n  workflow, data and distribution can still be valuable.\n- **Measure exposure:** what share of the product survives the provider doubling prices or withdrawing the\n  model, whether there is an abstraction layer, and how tightly prompts are tuned to one model.\n- **Price it in** as a remediation item, such as a gateway abstraction, rather than walking away."},{"q":"How would you stress-test a claimed 68% gross margin?","a":"- **Rebuild it from billing and traces:** cost per request and per customer, then project tokens per task,\n  retries, agent steps and human review at higher usage.\n- **Apply the roadmap:** a feature using 3× the tokens takes margin from about 68% to 22.5% at the same\n  price, before any discount lapses.\n- **Check pass-through:** whether pricing passes those costs on decides if this is a price adjustment."},{"q":"What AI-specific security questions do you add to the diligence review?","a":"- **Tenant isolation in retrieval,** including shared caches and indexes, because a cross-tenant leak in\n  an acquired product becomes the acquirer's incident.\n- **Agent blast radius** under prompt injection, prompt and output logging retention, and whether\n  third-party checkpoints are loaded in formats that can execute code."},{"q":"A target says its agent \"resolves 80% of tickets autonomously\". How do you test it?","a":"- **Pin the definition:** resolved must mean a checked end state, and autonomous must mean no human edited\n  or approved the action; read traces for review queues.\n- **Replay your tasks** in a sandbox with mock or read-only tools, report completion with its interval,\n  and compare steps and tokens per task against the target's own traces.\n- **Audit tool scopes** for blast radius under prompt injection, and price each gap as an adjustment or a\n  remediation item."}],"tradeoffs":["**Independent re-testing vs time.** Re-testing on your own data gives the most reliable evidence, but requires data-sharing agreements and time; when the timeline is short, a smaller sample still beats taking the claim on trust.","**Deep code and infrastructure review vs cost.** It finds operability risk but is expensive; scale it to how much of the deal value depends on running the system yourself rather than on its customers or team.","**Provider dependency as a red flag vs fair value.** Treating heavy reliance on a third-party model as a red flag protects against provider risk, but can undervalue a target whose real asset is workflow, data and distribution; judge the cost of switching, not the dependency itself.","**Strict findings vs reasonable deals.** Strict findings protect the buyer but can kill reasonable deals; classifying into deal-breaker, adjustment and remediation keeps the response proportionate.","**Sandbox replay vs realism.** Replaying agent tasks against mock tools is safe and repeatable, but mocks hide real API latency, errors and data quirks; where risk is high, add a small supervised run on real read-only systems."],"pitfalls":["**Accepting the target's accuracy number on its own eval set.** Symptom: after closing, quality drops on the acquirer's customers and nobody can explain why. Fix: your own held-out sample, by segment.","**Ignoring train/test overlap or prompt tuning on the eval set.** Scores look remarkably stable and high but fall sharply on any genuinely new sample.","**Computing gross margin at today's usage and today's discounted prices.** Margin erodes as features add tokens and the discount lapses.","**Skipping data-provenance questions.** A later customer audit or deletion request reveals the target cannot show the rights to its training data.","**Leaving the AI layer out of the security review.** A post-close penetration test finds one customer's documents retrievable by another through a shared index or response cache.","**Interviewing only founders.** Key-person risk and undocumented systems surface only when that person leaves after the earn-out period.","**Accepting an agent's \"resolution rate\" without its definition.** Symptom: after closing, customers reopen tickets the agent marked done. Fix: define completion as a checked end state and replay your own tasks before pricing."],"when":"- **Use for** any acquisition, investment or strategic partnership where AI is part of the value.\n- **Use a lighter form** for a critical vendor whose failure would hurt you (see vendor evaluation, which\n  covers the comparative selection process).\n- **Scale depth** to deal size and to how much value depends on the AI claims.\n- **For agent products, always include** a sandbox replay of your tasks, a tool-scope audit and cost per\n  completed task from traces.\n- **Outputs to aim for:** an independent accuracy number on your data broken down by segment, a dependency\n  and license map, an AI-layer security summary, a data-rights summary, a usage-sensitive margin model,\n  and a classified findings list.\n- **Interviews:** the strong signal is turning each headline claim into a test you would run.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs the independent eval on held-out data, reads traces to rebuild cost per request, and checks whether the system can actually be rebuilt and deployed from the repository.","architect":"Owns the dependency map, provider-exposure analysis and integration risk, and estimates the remediation needed to make the target fit the acquirer's platform and controls.","fde":"Brings real customer data and environments to the test, and judges whether the product will survive deployment in regulated or air-gapped customer settings."},"check":{"q":"A target claims a \"proprietary model\". How would you test that claim and why does the answer matter\nto the deal?","a":"I would ask for the production architecture and then verify it in traces and billing: which\nmodel calls are made, to which providers, with what weights or adapters. \"Proprietary\" might mean a model\ntrained from scratch, a fine-tune of an open-weight base, or prompts and retrieval around a third-party\nAPI. Each has different value and risk: a fine-tune needs data rights and license checks on the base\nmodel; a wrapper depends on provider pricing and terms, so its moat is workflow, data and distribution. I\nwould then measure how much better it is than a strong off-the-shelf baseline on our own held-out data.\nThe answer changes the valuation, not necessarily the decision."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the single most informative test in AI technical due diligence?","options":["Reviewing the target's architecture diagram","Running the system on a held-out sample the acquirer provides, scored by the acquirer's criteria","Counting the number of models the target has trained","Reading the target's published benchmark results"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Independent testing on data the target did not choose removes curation and contamination effects. A gap between their number and yours directly measures how far the headline claim transfers."},{"q":"Why can a healthy gross margin at current usage be misleading for an AI product?","options":["Gross margin excludes all infrastructure costs by definition","AI products always have negative margins at scale","Inference cost grows with tokens per task and usage, and may rest on temporary provider discounts","Revenue is always recognised later for AI products"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Features like longer context or multi-step agents multiply tokens per task, and discounts can lapse. Unless pricing passes those costs through, margin falls as usage grows."},{"q":"A target's eval set was partly used to tune its prompts. What symptom would you expect on fresh data?","options":["Higher accuracy than reported, because prompts generalise","Lower accuracy than reported, since the reported score partly reflects fitting to those examples","Identical accuracy, because prompt tuning does not affect evaluation","Random accuracy, because prompts are model-specific"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Iterating on prompts against an eval set fits the system to those examples, just like training on test data. The reported score overstates performance on genuinely new inputs."}]},{"key":"strategy/stakeholder-alignment","sources":[],"why":"- **More parties than a typical feature.** A business owner who wants a cost or speed gain, operators whose\n  workflow changes, legal and compliance (regulatory risk), security (data exposure), IT or the platform\n  team (must run it), finance (funds the inference bill), and sometimes works councils or unions. Each\n  defines success differently, and several have an effective veto.\n- **The naive approach aligns on the vision** (\"AI-assisted claims triage\") and starts building, expecting\n  details to settle later.\n- **The disagreements are about the numbers, not the vision.** Operations wants most claims handled\n  automatically; compliance wants zero wrongful denials; finance wants cost per claim below today's. With\n  a probabilistic system these trade off directly; unsettled up front, they get settled at launch, usually\n  by a late veto.\n- **The result is \"pilot purgatory\".** The demo impressed and the eval looks good to engineering, but there\n  was never an agreed bar, so nobody can say yes.\n- **The implicit standard of perfection.** Without an agreed baseline, any visible AI error is judged\n  against an imagined error-free human process rather than the real one.\n- **Scope:** this lesson is the up-front agreement; the ongoing relationship work is covered under\n  stakeholder management.","how":"### The method at a glance\nSteps 1-7 are the general alignment method for any AI system: map the people, separate goals from red\nlines, measure the human baseline, choose an operating point on a measured curve, pre-wire, write one page\nand re-align at gates. Step 8 is what an agent adds.\n\n### 1. Map who matters and what each can do\n| Column | What to record |\n|---|---|\n| Stake | What they gain or lose |\n| Power | Fund, approve, veto, adopt, block quietly |\n| Main fear | What would make them say no |\n\n- **Pay most attention to those who can veto late:** legal, security, data protection, and frontline users\n  whose non-adoption is a silent veto.\n- **Engage them first**, *because* objections raised before design change a requirement; raised after\n  build, they change a plan.\n\n### 2. Elicit success criteria and red lines separately\n- **Ask each stakeholder two questions:** what outcome they need (a metric), and what they cannot accept\n  (a constraint).\n- **Constraints are hard limits:** \"no customer data leaves the EU\", \"no automated denial without human\n  review\".\n- **Outcomes are things to optimise.**\n- **Keep them separate** so a constraint is not traded away in a negotiation over an outcome.\n\n### 3. Measure the baseline\n- **Measure the current process on the same metrics:** human error rate, cycle time, cost per item.\n- **This replaces \"AI must never be wrong\"** with \"AI plus review must beat today's process by an agreed\n  margin\", a standard a system can actually meet.\n- **Measure on the *same items* the AI will handle**, *because* a system that automates only its confident\n  cases takes the easier slice of the work. Humans are usually more accurate on that slice than their\n  overall rate suggests, so comparing the AI's error on its slice with humans' error on everything flatters\n  the AI.\n- **Historical decisions on the labelled eval set** usually give you this for free.\n\n### 4. Agree the operating point with evidence\n- **Run an offline eval on representative data** and show the trade-off curve: as the confidence threshold\n  for automation rises, fewer items are automated and fewer are wrong.\n- **Stakeholders choose a point on a real curve** instead of arguing in the abstract. This is the\n  AI-specific heart of alignment.\n\n### 5. Pre-wire before the decision meeting\n- **Walk each veto holder through the evidence one-to-one first**, *because* an objection raised privately\n  can be met with a design change, while one raised first in front of peers tends to harden into a\n  position.\n- **The group meeting ratifies** a choice everyone has already seen.\n- **Remaining disagreement is named and escalated** to the agreed decision owner rather than re-argued.\n\n### 6. Write it down in one page\n| Section | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Goal | The outcome in one line |\n| Primary metric | Baseline and target |\n| Hard constraints | The red lines |\n| Operating point | The chosen threshold and why |\n| Failure handling | Fallback to humans, escalation path, rollback trigger |\n| Decision rights | Who approves launch, who can pause it (a RACI or DACI model works) |\n| Review cadence | When the agreement is revisited |\n\nA written artefact matters *because* memories of verbal agreements diverge within weeks.\n\n### 7. Re-align at each gate\n- **Present results against the one-pager** at each roadmap gate.\n- **If results move the trade-off** (accuracy lower than hoped, costs higher), re-open the decision\n  explicitly rather than drifting.\n\n### 8. What an agent adds to the one-pager\n| Section | What to agree for an agent |\n|---|---|\n| Action classes | Which tool calls are read-only, reversible or irreversible, and which run without approval |\n| Approval gates | Who approves consequential actions, and the limit (amount, scope) above which approval is required |\n| Escalation threshold | When the agent hands off to a human: low confidence, step budget hit, or a red-line topic |\n| Budgets | Steps and spend per task, so a looping agent stops before it costs real money |\n| Kill switch | Who can pause the agent, how fast, and what happens to tasks in flight |\n\n- **Why each needs an owner:** these are the decisions a veto holder would otherwise make at launch.","example":"### Worked scenario: claims triage with three conflicting goals\nFigures are illustrative.\n\n- **Operations VP:** wants at least 50% of claims routed automatically.\n- **Compliance:** red line, wrong-queue errors on auto-routed claims must be no worse than today's manual\n  error rate, which is 4% across all claims.\n- **Finance:** cost per claim below today's $3.10.\n\nThe team runs the classifier on 1,000 labelled historical claims and tabulates by confidence threshold:\n\n| Threshold | Share auto-routed | Error rate on auto-routed | Blended cost per claim |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.70 | 78% | 6.5% | $1.20 |\n| 0.85 | 61% | 3.2% | $1.75 |\n| 0.95 | 38% | 1.1% | $2.30 |\n\n- **At 0.70** ops gets its volume but the compliance red line is breached.\n- **At 0.95** compliance is comfortable but ops is well short of 50%.\n- **At 0.85** all three look satisfied: 61% automated, 3.2% error, cost well under $3.10.\n\nTwo checks make the margin thinner than it looks:\n1. **The fair baseline.** The labelled claims carry their original human routing. On the same 610 claims\n   the model would automate, humans erred 3.5% of the time, not 4%; on the 390 harder claims they erred\n   about 4.8% (0.61 × 3.5% + 0.39 × 4.8% ≈ 4.0%).\n   - So the model is roughly at parity on its slice, and the whole process improves only from about 4.0%\n     to 0.61 × 3.2% + 0.39 × 4.8% ≈ 3.8%.\n2. **Noise.** 3.2% of 610 is roughly 20 errors, and its 95% interval runs to about 4.6%.\n\nThe outcome:\n- **Compliance agrees parity is acceptable**, since the red line was \"no worse\".\n- **The one-pager records 0.85 as a provisional operating point**, with a rollback trigger if the\n  production error rate over a rolling audited sample (large enough, a few hundred claims or more, that\n  noise alone does not trip it) exceeds 4%, compliance as the owner of that trigger, and a review after a\n  month of production data.\n- **The argument that would have happened at launch** happened in a one-hour meeting with a table on the\n  screen.\n\n### Checking the operating point and the rollback trigger in code\nThe table, the fair baseline and the noise check as one script, plus a question the one-pager should\nanswer: how often would the 4% trigger fire from noise alone if the true error stayed at 3.2%?\n\n```python\nfrom math import comb, sqrt\n\nrows = [  # threshold, share auto-routed, error on auto-routed, blended cost per claim\n    (0.70, 0.78, 0.065, 1.20),\n    (0.85, 0.61, 0.032, 1.75),\n    (0.95, 0.38, 0.011, 2.30),\n]\nMIN_AUTO, MAX_ERR, MAX_COST = 0.50, 0.04, 3.10      # ops goal, compliance red line, finance goal\nfor t, share, err, cost in rows:\n    ok = share >= MIN_AUTO and err <= MAX_ERR and cost < MAX_COST\n    print(f\"threshold {t:.2f}: auto {share:.0%} err {err:.1%} ${cost:.2f} ->\", \"candidate\" if ok else \"fails\")\n\nshare, err, n_auto = 0.61, 0.032, 610\nhuman_easy, human_hard = 0.035, 0.048                # humans on the same 610 claims, and on the other 390\nprint(f\"fair baseline {share * human_easy + (1 - share) * human_hard:.1%}, \"\n      f\"with model {share * err + (1 - share) * human_hard:.1%}\")\nprint(f\"~{err * n_auto:.0f} errors, upper 95% bound {err + 1.96 * sqrt(err * (1 - err) / n_auto):.1%}\")\n\ndef false_alarm(n, p_true=0.032, trigger=0.04):      # P(observed rate > trigger) from noise alone\n    k = int(trigger * n)\n    return 1 - sum(comb(n, i) * p_true**i * (1 - p_true)**(n - i) for i in range(k + 1))\n\nfor n in (50, 300, 1000):\n    print(f\"audit sample {n:>4}: false rollback chance {false_alarm(n):.0%}\")\n# threshold 0.70: auto 78% err 6.5% $1.20 -> fails\n# threshold 0.85: auto 61% err 3.2% $1.75 -> candidate\n# threshold 0.95: auto 38% err 1.1% $2.30 -> fails\n# fair baseline 4.0%, with model 3.8%\n# ~20 errors, upper 95% bound 4.6%\n# audit sample   50: false rollback chance 21%\n# audit sample  300: false rollback chance 17%\n# audit sample 1000: false rollback chance 7%\n```\n\n- **The trigger sits close to the operating error** (4% vs 3.2%), so even a few hundred audited claims\n  leave a meaningful chance of a false rollback; larger windows cut it.\n- **Agree the window size and the response** (pause and re-audit, or immediate fallback) in the one-pager,\n  so a noisy reading does not restart the negotiation.\n\n### Worked scenario: agreeing a refund agent's approval limit\nA support agent can issue refunds through a tool. Operations wants a short approval queue; finance's red\nline is wrong-refund cost under $1,000 per month. The team replays a month of tickets through the agent\noffline and projects each limit. All figures are illustrative.\n\n| Auto-approve limit | Refunds the agent issues alone | Projected wrong-refund cost per month | Approvals queued per day |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $25 | 45% | $300 | 120 |\n| $50 | 70% | $900 | 66 |\n| $200 | 92% | $4,800 | 18 |\n\n- **$200 breaches finance's red line; $25 swamps the approvers.** $50 meets both with little margin.\n- **The one-pager records** $50 as provisional, finance as owner of the wrong-refund trigger, a rolling\n  audit of auto-issued refunds, and operations holding the kill switch.\n- **The engineer implements exactly that:** the limit in config, an approval gate on the refund tool above\n  it, and an audit sample of the rest.","patterns":["**One-page decision record in version control** — store the one-pager (goal, metric with baseline and target, red lines, operating point, rollback trigger, owners, review date) next to the system's eval config, and link it from the launch approval. Monitor whether each gate decision cites it.","**Threshold as configuration, owned by the decision owner** — keep the confidence threshold (0.85 here) in config rather than code, so moving the operating point is a recorded change approved by whoever the one-pager names. Monitor automation share and error rate per threshold change.","**Rolling audited sample with a sized window** — route a random sample of auto-routed items to human audit continuously, and size the window from the false-alarm calculation above. Monitor the audited error rate against the trigger, and audit backlog age.","**Same-slice baseline from historical labels** — compute the human error rate on exactly the items the model would automate, using the original human decisions in the labelled set. Report it beside the model's error at every gate.","**Pre-wire log** — before each decision meeting, record which veto holders saw the evidence one-to-one and what they raised. Monitor objections that first appear in the group meeting; each one is a missed pre-wire.","**Agreed limits as agent config** — put approval limits, escalation thresholds, step and spend budgets per task in versioned config that names its owner from the one-pager. Monitor actions taken above each limit (should be zero) and the approval-queue length.","**Owned kill switch** — give the named owner a tested way to pause the agent or drop it to draft-only mode without a deploy. Monitor time to pause in drills and tasks left in flight when it fires."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent takes actions, so the late veto is sharper: compliance, security\n  and finance each care which tool calls it may make alone. Without agreement up front, a working agent\n  sits in pilot because nobody agreed what it may do.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The agreement becomes configuration you implement: the escalation\n  threshold, the approval gate per tool or action class, refund or spend limits, the audit sample and the\n  kill switch with a named owner.\n- **What you control.** Producing the evidence (a threshold trade-off table from your agent eval, a\n  same-slice human baseline) and putting every agreed limit in config, not code.\n- **What you can skip.** Running the stakeholder meetings and change-management detail; know what goes on\n  the one-pager so you can build exactly what it says.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Launching a customer-facing agent.** Support, compliance and finance agree which replies and refunds\n  the agent sends alone before the first line of agent code.\n- **Unblocking an agent stuck in pilot.** A one-pager with a replay-based trade-off table turns \"is it good\n  enough?\" into choosing a limit everyone has seen.\n- **Raising an agent's autonomy.** Audited evidence at the current limit is presented against the\n  one-pager to justify a higher approval limit.\n- **Handling an agent incident.** The named kill-switch owner drops the agent to draft-only mode without a\n  meeting, because the response was agreed in advance.","mini_project":"**Alignment one-pager and config for a refund agent (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn three stakeholders' goals into an agreed agent limit and the config that enforces it.\n- **Steps:** write each stakeholder's outcome and red line; simulate 200 refund requests with amounts and\n  a mock agent correctness rate; tabulate share auto-issued, projected wrong-refund cost and queue length\n  for three limits; pick the operating point; write the one-pager with action classes, owners and kill\n  switch; express the limits as a config file and a gate function the refund tool calls.\n- **Done when:** the table, the one-pager and a test showing a refund above the limit is routed to approval\n  all agree, and pausing the agent switches it to draft-only."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"Map stakeholders, power, fears\"] --> E[\"Elicit outcomes and red lines\"]\n    E --> B[\"Baseline on the same items\"]\n    B --> C[\"Offline eval: threshold trade-off curve\"]\n    C --> PW[\"Pre-wire veto holders one-to-one\"]\n    PW --> R[\"Meeting ratifies operating point\"]\n    R --> OP[\"One-pager: metric, red lines, rollback, owners\"]\n    OP --> P[\"Agent in production, audited sample\"]\n    P -->|\"rollback trigger hit\"| RB[\"Fall back to humans\"]\n    P -->|\"each gate\"| G{\"Trade-off moved?\"}\n    G -->|\"yes\"| C\n    G -->|\"no\"| P","caption":"Alignment is a sequence: constraints and a fair baseline come before the trade-off curve, and veto holders\nsee the evidence privately before the meeting that ratifies the agent's operating point and limits."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is stakeholder alignment harder for AI systems than for deterministic features?","a":"- **The goals trade off on one curve.** Automation rate, error rate and cost all move with the confidence\n  threshold, so stakeholders must agree on an operating point rather than on a feature spec.\n- **Without that agreement,** a pilot can look good to engineering and still be unapprovable, because\n  nobody agreed what \"good enough\" meant."},{"q":"Your pilot met every target, but a compliance lead blocks launch in the final review. What went wrong and what do you do now?","a":"- **Diagnosis:** the veto holder was engaged late or not pre-wired, so the objection surfaced in front of\n  peers and hardened into a position.\n- **Now:** meet one-to-one, turn the objection into a red line or a design change (a human review gate, a\n  lower threshold), and re-run the eval at the new operating point.\n- **Next time:** map veto holders first and walk them through evidence before any group decision."},{"q":"How do you set a fair human baseline when the model only automates its confident cases?","a":"- **Measure humans on the same items** the model would automate, using historical decisions in the\n  labelled set, because confident cases are usually easier and humans do better on them too.\n- **In the claims example,** humans erred 3.5% on the automated slice versus 4% overall, turning an\n  apparent win into rough parity and a whole-process gain from about 4.0% to 3.8%."},{"q":"When would you skip a formal alignment process?","a":"- **For internal experiments that are cheap to stop,** a lighter agreement on the stop condition is enough,\n  because the cost of a late objection is small.\n- **Never skip it** when the system changes a workflow, touches regulated data, or needs sign-off from\n  people outside the building team, because those are exactly the late vetoes."},{"q":"How would you design the rollback trigger for an agent or automated decision system?","a":"- **Tie it to the red line:** for example, audited production error on auto-routed items above 4%, with a\n  named owner (compliance in the claims case) and a defined response.\n- **Size the audit window** so noise alone rarely trips it; with true error at 3.2% and a 4% trigger, even\n  300 audited claims give a false rollback about 17% of the time.\n- **For an agent, define the fallback mode:** drop to draft-only or approval-on-every-action rather than\n  switching off, so work continues while the owner re-audits."},{"q":"Your agent can issue refunds and update accounts. What must stakeholders agree before you build it?","a":"- **Action classes:** which tool calls are read-only, reversible or irreversible, and which may run\n  without a human.\n- **Limits and owners:** the approval limit per action class from an offline replay, the escalation\n  threshold, step and spend budgets per task, and who owns each trigger.\n- **The kill switch:** who can pause the agent or drop it to draft-only, and how in-flight tasks are\n  handled; then implement all of it as config."}],"tradeoffs":["**Up-front alignment vs speed.** It prevents late vetoes but delays the start; for a reversible internal experiment, a lighter agreement on the stop condition is enough.","**Many stakeholders vs fast decisions.** Involving many surfaces constraints early but slows decisions; give advisory voice broadly and decision rights narrowly.","**Explicit numeric bars vs flexibility.** They make go/no-go clear but commit you before the data is fully understood; set them as provisional and re-open at each gate with evidence.","**Showing the full error trade-off vs sponsor comfort.** It builds trust with risk owners but exposes failure rates some sponsors would rather not see; hiding them only moves the discovery to launch, where it costs more.","**Tight approval gates vs agent usefulness.** Approving every action keeps risk owners comfortable but turns the agent into a drafting tool with a queue; loosen gates per action class as audited evidence accumulates."],"pitfalls":["**Aligning on vision instead of numbers.** Symptom: a successful pilot that nobody can approve because nobody agreed what \"good enough\" meant. Fix: an agreed operating point.","**Engaging legal, security or data protection only before launch.** Symptom: a late veto or a redesign around a constraint that was knowable on day one.","**No baseline for the human process.** Any visible AI error is compared with perfection and the project stalls on anecdotes.","**Treating frontline users as recipients rather than stakeholders.** Adoption stays low while every dashboard looks green, a silent veto.","**Comparing the AI's error on the cases it chose to automate with humans' error on all cases.** The launch clears the bar, and a later audit shows the automated slice is worse than humans were on those same, easier claims.","**Decision rights left implicit.** When production metrics wobble, two executives give contradictory instructions and the team freezes.","**Agreeing the agent's goal but not its actions.** Symptom: security blocks launch on discovering the agent can send email or issue refunds alone. Fix: put action classes, approval limits and the kill-switch owner on the one-pager before build."],"when":"- **Use before significant build work** on any AI system that changes a workflow, touches regulated data,\n  or needs sign-off from people outside the building team, which is most of them.\n- **Revisit** at each roadmap gate and whenever evidence moves the trade-off.\n- **Skip the heavy version** for internal experiments that are cheap to stop.\n- **Never skip it for agents that write** to systems of record, move money or message customers; agree\n  action classes and approval limits first.\n- **Measure alignment** by whether a launch decision can be made from the one-pager and the latest eval\n  without a new negotiation.\n- **Interviews:** describe turning conflicting goals into a choice on a measured trade-off curve.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the threshold trade-off table and the baseline measurement that let stakeholders choose an operating point with evidence.","architect":"Translates stakeholders' red lines into hard design constraints (residency, human review gates, audit trails) and owns the decision-rights section.","fde":"Runs the alignment meetings inside the customer, identifies the quiet vetoes early, and keeps the one-pager current as the deployment evolves."},"check":{"q":"The business sponsor says the AI must be \"100% accurate\" before launch. How do you move the\nconversation forward?","a":"I would not argue with the goal; I would make the comparison concrete. First measure the current\nprocess: how often human handlers make the same kind of error, and what that costs. Then show the eval\nresults as a trade-off: at a given confidence threshold, what share of items the system handles, its error\nrate on those, and what happens to the rest (human review). The question becomes \"which operating point\nbeats today's process, with which safeguards\", plus a rollback trigger if production errors exceed the\nbaseline. Most sponsors accept a measured improvement with a safety net once they see the human error\nrate."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why separate stakeholders' hard constraints from their outcome goals?","options":["Constraints are only relevant to legal teams","So a red line cannot be traded away during negotiation over metrics that are meant to be optimised","Outcomes are always less important than constraints","Constraints are easier to measure than outcomes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Outcomes such as automation rate are legitimately traded against each other; constraints such as data residency are not. Mixing them invites treating a red line as one more weight in the balance."},{"q":"What makes alignment on AI systems harder than on deterministic features?","options":["AI systems need more stakeholders by regulation","Stakeholders cannot understand model architectures","Goals like automation rate and error rate trade off directly, so an operating point must be chosen","AI systems cannot be measured before deployment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A probabilistic system is right only part of the time, so \"does it work\" depends on the chosen threshold. Stakeholders with different goals must agree on a point on that trade-off curve."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of measuring the human baseline before deployment?","options":["To show that the human team should be reduced","To train the model on human mistakes","To replace an implicit perfection standard with a realistic bar the system can be judged against","To satisfy a requirement of most model providers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without a baseline, every AI error is compared with an imagined error-free process. Measuring today's error rate and cost gives an agreed, achievable bar for the go/no-go decision."}]}]});
